window.BAHR_DATA=window.BAHR_DATA||{};window.BAHR_DATA["passages-11"]=[{"id":"q2-186","q":"2:186","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:186 · Divine nearness, answered prayer and transcendence beyond place","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 213–215","cairo":"I:213–215","source_page_first":213,"source_page_last":215,"ar":"وَإِذا سَأَلَكَ عِبادِي عَنِّي فَإِنِّي قَرِيبٌ أُجِيبُ دَعْوَةَ الدَّاعِ إِذا دَعانِ فَلْيَسْتَجِيبُوا لِي وَلْيُؤْمِنُوا بِي لَعَلَّهُمْ يَرْشُدُونَ (186)\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: في جواب رجل سأل: هل قريب ربنا فنناجيَه، أو بعيد فنناديه؟ فنزل: وَإِذا سَأَلَكَ عِبادِي عَنِّي. فقل لهم: فَإِنِّي قَرِيبٌ إليهم من أرواحهم لأشباحهم، ومن وَسْواس قلوبهم لقلوبهم، عِلْماً وقدرة وإحاطة، أجيب دعوة الداعي إذا دَعَانِ، سرّاً أو جهراً، ليلاً أو نهاراً، على ما يليق بحاله في الوقت الذي نريد، لا في الوقت الذي يريد، فَلْيَسْتَجِيبُوا لِي إذا دعوتُهم للإيمان والطاعة، أَسْلُك بهم طريق المعرفة، وَلْيُؤْمِنُوا بِي إني قريب منهم فَيَسْتَحْيُوا مني، حياءَ مَنْ يرى أني معه حيث كان، لَعَلَّهُمْ يَرْشُدُونَ إلى سلوك طريقتي ودوام محبتي.\nقال البيضاوي: اعلم أنه، تعالى، لما أمرهم بصوم الشهر، ومراعاة العدة على القيام بوظائف التكبير والشكر، عقَّبه بهذه الآية الدالّة على أنه خبير بأحوالهم، سميع لأقوالهم مجيب لدعائهم، مجازيهم على أعمالهم، تأكيداً وحثّاً عليه. هـ.\nالإشارة: قُرْب الحقّ تعالى من عباده هو قرب المعاني من المحسوسات، أو قرب الصفات من الذات، أو الذات من الصفات. فإذا تحقق المحو والاضمحلال، وزال البَيْن، وثبت الوصال، لم يبقَ قرب ولا بعد ولا بَينٌ ولا انفصال. قال الشيخ القطبُ العارف الكبير سيدي عبد السلام بن مشيش رضي الله عنه لأبى الحسن رضي الله عنه: حدِّدْ بصرَ الإيمان تَجد الله في كل شيء وعند كل شيء، ومع كل شيء، وقبل كل شيء، وبعد كل شيء، وقريباً من كل شيء، ومحيطاً بكل شيء، بقُرب هو وصَفْهُ، وبحيطة هي نعتُه، وَعَدّ عن الظرفية والحدود، وعن الأماكن والجهات، وعن الصحبة والقرب في المسافات، وعن الدَّوْر بالمخلوقات، وامْحَق الكلَّ بوصفه الأول والآخر والظاهر والباطن، «كان الله ولا شيء معه، وهو الآن على ما عليه كان» .\nوقال بعض العارفين: الحق تعالى منزّه عن الأين والجهة والكيف والمادة والصورة. ومع ذلك لا يخلو منه أين ولا مكان، ولا كم ولا كيف، ولا جسم، ولا جوهر ولا عرض، لأنه للطفه سارٍ في كل شيء، ولنوريته ظاهر في كل شيء، ولإطلاقه وإحاطته متكيّف بكل كيف، غير متقيد بذلك، فمن لم يعرف هذا ولم يذقه ولم يشهده فهو أعمى البصيرة، محروم من مشاهدة الحق تعالى. هـ.\nوهذه الإشارات لا يفهمها إلا أهل الذَّوْق من أهل المعاني، فاصحب الرجال أهلَ المعاني تِذُقْ أسرارهم، وتفهم إشاراتهم. وإلا فحسبُك أن تعتقد كمال التنزيه، وبطلان التشبيه، وتَمَسَّكْ بقوله تعالى: لَيْسَ كَمِثْلِهِ شَيْءٌ وَهُوَ السَّمِيعُ الْبَصِيرُ، وسلَّمْ للرجال في كل حال.\nإِنْ لم تَر الهلالَ فسَلِّم ... لأناس رأوه بالأبْصَار\nوإذا تحققت أن الحقّ قريب منك كفاك لسانُ الحال عن طلب المقال، وبالله التوفيق.\nثم تمّم الحق تعالى بقية أحكام الصوم، فقال:","quran_ar":"وَإِذا سَأَلَكَ عِبادِي عَنِّي فَإِنِّي قَرِيبٌ أُجِيبُ دَعْوَةَ الدَّاعِ إِذا دَعانِ فَلْيَسْتَجِيبُوا لِي وَلْيُؤْمِنُوا بِي لَعَلَّهُمْ يَرْشُدُونَ (186)","quran_en":"186. When My servants ask you about Me, I am near. I answer the supplicant’s call when he calls upon Me. Let them answer Me and believe in Me, so that they may be rightly directed.","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"The answer in the time God wills","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says, in answer to a man asking, “Is our Lord near, so that we converse quietly with Him, or distant, so that we call aloud?”: when My servants ask you about Me, tell them I am nearer to them, in knowledge, power and encompassing, than their spirits to their bodies and the whispers of their hearts to their hearts. I answer the caller, secretly or aloud, by night or day, in the way fitting his condition and at the time We will, not the time he wills. Let them answer Me when I call to faith and obedience, and I shall lead them on gnosis’s path. Let them believe I am near, feeling shame before Me as one who sees that I am with him wherever he is, so that they are directed to travel My path and persist in loving Me.<\/p><p>Al-Bayḍāwī says: after commanding the month’s fast, observing its count and performing the duties of magnification and thanks, God follows with this verse. It shows that He knows their conditions, hears their words, answers their prayers and repays their deeds, reinforcing and encouraging the command.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says, in answer to a man asking, “Is our Lord near, so that we converse quietly with Him, or distant, so that we call aloud?”: when My servants ask you about Me, tell them I am nearer to them, in knowledge, power and encompassing, than their spirits to their bodies and the whispers of their hearts to their hearts. I answer the caller, secretly or aloud, by night or day, in the way fitting his condition and at the time We will, not the time he wills. Let them answer Me when I call to faith and obedience, and I shall lead them on gnosis’s path. Let them believe I am near, feeling shame before Me as one who sees that I am with him wherever he is, so that they are directed to travel My path and persist in loving Me. Al-Bayḍāwī says: after commanding the month’s fast, observing its count and performing the duties of magnification and thanks, God follows with this verse. It shows that He knows their conditions, hears their words, answers their prayers and repays their deeds, reinforcing and encouraging the command."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Nearness without spatial proximity","html":"<p>The Real’s nearness to His servants is the nearness of meanings to sensory things, or Attributes to Essence, or Essence to Attributes. When effacement and disappearance are realized, separation removed and union established, neither nearness nor distance, between-ness nor separation remains.<\/p><p>The great gnostic and pole, Sīdī ʿAbd al-Salām ibn Mashīsh, told Abū al-Ḥasan: “Sharpen faith’s vision and you will find God in everything, at everything, with everything, before everything, after everything, near everything and encompassing everything—with a nearness that is His attribute and an encompassing that is His quality. Pass beyond containment and limits, places and directions, companionship and nearness measured by distances, and circling among created things. Efface everything through His attributes ‘the First, the Last, the Outward, the Inward.’ God was, and nothing was with Him; He is now as He was.”<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-125\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B120]<\/a><\/p><p>A gnostic said: “The Real transcends where, direction, how, matter and form. Yet no where or place, quantity or quality, body, substance or accident is empty of Him. Through His subtlety He pervades everything; through His luminosity He appears in everything; through His absoluteness and encompassing He assumes every modality without being restricted by it. Whoever has neither known, tasted nor witnessed this is blind in inward sight, deprived of witnessing the Real.”<\/p><p>Only people of spiritual taste and inward meanings understand these allusions. Accompany the men of meanings, and you will taste their secrets and understand their allusions. Otherwise it suffices to affirm perfect transcendence and the falsity of assimilation, holding to “Nothing is like Him, and He is the Hearing, the Seeing” [42:11], and yielding to these men in every state.<\/p><blockquote class=\"poetry\">If you have not seen the crescent, yield<br>to people who have seen it with their eyes.<\/blockquote><p>When you realize that the Real is near, the tongue of the state (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">لسان الحال<\/bdi>) suffices in place of verbal petition. Through God is success.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"The Real’s nearness to His servants is the nearness of meanings to sensory things, or Attributes to Essence, or Essence to Attributes. When effacement and disappearance are realized, separation removed and union established, neither nearness nor distance, between-ness nor separation remains. The great gnostic and pole, Sīdī ʿAbd al-Salām ibn Mashīsh, told Abū al-Ḥasan: “Sharpen faith’s vision and you will find God in everything, at everything, with everything, before everything, after everything, near everything and encompassing everything—with a nearness that is His attribute and an encompassing that is His quality. Pass beyond containment and limits, places and directions, companionship and nearness measured by distances, and circling among created things. Efface everything through His attributes ‘the First, the Last, the Outward, the Inward.’ God was, and nothing was with Him; He is now as He was.” [B120] A gnostic said: “The Real transcends where, direction, how, matter and form. Yet no where or place, quantity or quality, body, substance or accident is empty of Him. Through His subtlety He pervades everything; through His luminosity He appears in everything; through His absoluteness and encompassing He assumes every modality without being restricted by it. Whoever has neither known, tasted nor witnessed this is blind in inward sight, deprived of witnessing the Real.” Only people of spiritual taste and inward meanings understand these allusions. Accompany the men of meanings, and you will taste their secrets and understand their allusions. Otherwise it suffices to affirm perfect transcendence and the falsity of assimilation, holding to “Nothing is like Him, and He is the Hearing, the Seeing” [42:11], and yielding to these men in every state. If you have not seen the crescent, yield to people who have seen it with their eyes. When you realize that the Real is near, the tongue of the state ( لسان الحال ) suffices in place of verbal petition. Through God is success."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>The Real completes the remaining rules of fasting:<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real completes the remaining rules of fasting:"}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":3110,"arabic_characters":2389},{"id":"q2-187","q":"2:187","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:187 · The nights of fasting, the dawn’s discernment and retreat","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 215–218","cairo":"I:215–218","source_page_first":215,"source_page_last":218,"ar":"أُحِلَّ لَكُمْ لَيْلَةَ الصِّيامِ الرَّفَثُ إِلى نِسائِكُمْ هُنَّ لِباسٌ لَكُمْ وَأَنْتُمْ لِباسٌ لَهُنَّ عَلِمَ اللَّهُ أَنَّكُمْ كُنْتُمْ تَخْتانُونَ أَنْفُسَكُمْ فَتابَ عَلَيْكُمْ وَعَفا عَنْكُمْ فَالْآنَ بَاشِرُوهُنَّ وَابْتَغُوا ما كَتَبَ اللَّهُ لَكُمْ وَكُلُوا وَاشْرَبُوا حَتَّى يَتَبَيَّنَ لَكُمُ الْخَيْطُ الْأَبْيَضُ مِنَ الْخَيْطِ الْأَسْوَدِ مِنَ الْفَجْرِ ثُمَّ أَتِمُّوا الصِّيامَ إِلَى اللَّيْلِ وَلا تُبَاشِرُوهُنَّ وَأَنْتُمْ عاكِفُونَ فِي الْمَساجِدِ تِلْكَ حُدُودُ اللَّهِ فَلا تَقْرَبُوها كَذلِكَ يُبَيِّنُ اللَّهُ آياتِهِ لِلنَّاسِ لَعَلَّهُمْ يَتَّقُونَ (187)\nقلت: الرفَث: مُحَرِّك الجِماع، والفُحْش كالرفوث، وكلام النساء في الجماع. قاله في القاموس، وقال الأزهري اللغوي: الرفَث: كلمة جامعة لكل ما يريد الرجل من امرأته، وضمَّنه هنا الإفضاء، فعدَّاه بإلى.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله في نسخ ما كان في أول الإسلام من تحريم الجِماع في رمضان بعد العشاء أو النوم، ثم إن عمر رضي الله عنه باشر امرأته بعد العشاء، فندم وأتى رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم يعتذر إليه، فقام رجال فاعترفوا بما صنعوا بعد العشاء فنزل قوله: أُحِلَّ لَكُمْ لَيْلَةَ الصِّيامِ قبل الفجر، الإفضاءُ إِلى نِسائِكُمْ بالجماع. وعبَّر بالرفث تقبيحاً لما ارتكبوه.\nثم علَّل التحليل بقوله: هُنَّ لِباسٌ لَكُمْ وَأَنْتُمْ لِباسٌ لَهُنَّ، أي: وإنما أبحتُ لكم الجماع لقلّة صبركم عليهن، حتى تعانقوهن ويعانقنكم، فيشتمل بعضُكم على بعض، كاشتمال اللباس على صاحبه، كما قال الشاعر:\nإذَا مَا الضَّجِيعَ ثَنَى عِطْفَهَا ... تَثَنَّتْ فكَانَتْ عَلَيْهِ لِبَاسَا\nوهذه الحالة يقلُّ فيها الصبرُ عن الوقَاع، عَلِمَ اللَّهُ أَنَّكُمْ كُنْتُمْ تَخْتانُونَ أَنْفُسَكُمْ أي تَخُونُونها فَتُعرِّضُونها للعقاب، وتَحْرمُونها من الثواب، فَتابَ عَلَيْكُمْ لَمّا تُبْتُم واعترفتم بما اقْتَرَفْتُم، وعفا عنكم فمحا ذنوبكم، فَالْآنَ بَاشِرُوهُنَّ. والمباشرة: إلصاق البَشْرة بالبشرة، كنايةً عن الجماع، وَابْتَغُوا ما كَتَبَ اللَّهُ لَكُمْ\nمن النسل، فلا تباشروهن لمجرد قضاء الشهوة، بل اطلبوا ما قدَّر الله لكم، وأثْبتَه في اللوح المحفوظ من الولد، لأنه هو المقصود من تشريع النكاح، وخلق الشهوة، لا مجرد قضاء الوطر. وفي الحديث: «إِذَا مَاتَ العبدُ انْقَطَعَ عَمَلُه إِلاَّ مَن ثَلاثٍَ: صَدَقَةٍ جَاريَةٍ، وعِلْمٍ بثَّه في صُدور الرِّجالِ، وولدٍ صالح يدعو لَهُ» .\nوفي حديث طويل عن عائشة- رضي الله عنها- في قصة الحَوْلاء- امرأة من الأنصار-، قال لها رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم: ما من امرأة حمَلَت من زوجها حين تَحْمِل، إلا لها من الأجْر مثل القائم ليلَة الصائم نهارَه، والغازي في سبيل الله، وما من امرأة يأتيها الطَلْقُ، إلا كان لها بكل طَلْقةٍ عِتْق نسمةٍ، وبكل رَضْعة عتق رقبة، فإذا فَطَمت ولَدها ناداها مُنادِ من السماء: قد كُفيتِ العملَ فيما مضى، فاستأنفي العمل فيما بقي. قالت عائشة- رضي الله عنها-: قد أُعْطِي النساءُ خيراً كثيراً، فما لكم يا معشر الرجال؟. فضحك النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم ثم قال: ما من رجل مؤمن أخذ بيد امرأتِه يُرَاوِدُها، إلا كتب الله له حسنة، وإنْ عانقها فعشْر حسنات، وإن ضاجعها فعشرون حسنةٌ، وإن أتاها كان خيراً من الدنيا وما فيها، فإذا قام ليغتسل لم يمر الماء على شعرة من جسده إلا مُحِيَ عنه سيئة، ويُعطى له درجة، وما يعطى بغسله خير من الدنيا وما فيها، وإن الله تعالى يباهي الملائكة فيقول: انظروا إلى عبدي قام في ليلة قَرَّة يغتسل من الجنابة، يتيقن بأني ربه، اشهدوا أني قد غفرت له» . هـ. من الثعلبي.\nثم أباح الحق تعالى الأكلَ والشرب، ليلة الصيام إلى الفجر، فقال: وَكُلُوا وَاشْرَبُوا حَتَّى يَتَبَيَّنَ لَكُمُ الْخَيْطُ الْأَبْيَضُ مِنَ الْخَيْطِ الْأَسْوَدِ شبَّه أول ما يبدو من الفجر المعترِض في الأفق، بالخيط الأبيض، وما يمتد معه من غَبَشِ الليل، بالخيط الأسود.\nولم ينزل قوله تعالى: مِنَ الْفَجْرِ إلا بعد مدة، فحمله بعض الصحابة على ظاهره، فعمد إلى خيطٍ أبيض وخيط أسود فجعلهما تحت وِسادته، فجعل يأكل وينظر إليهما، فلم يتبيَّنا، ومنهم عَديُّ بنُ حاتم، قال: فغدوتُ إلى رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم فأخبرته فضحِك، وقال: «إِنك لَعَرِيضُ القَفا، إِنَّمَا ذلِكَ بَيَاضُ النَّهَارِ وسَوَادُ اللَّيْلِ» ، والحديث ثابت في البخاري وغيرِه. واعترضه الزمخشري بأن فيه تأخير البيان عن وقت الحاجة، وذلك لا يجوز، لما فيه من التكليف بما لا يطاق.\nوأُجيب بأنه ليس فيه تأخير البيان عن وقت الحاجة، وإنما فيه تأخير البيان لوقت الحاجة، وهو جائز. وبيان ذلك أنه لمّا نزل قوله تعالى: حَتَّى يَتَبَيَّنَ لَكُمُ الْخَيْطُ الْأَبْيَضُ مِنَ الْخَيْطِ الْأَسْوَدِ فهم رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم والمؤمنون مُرادَ الله منهما، واستمر عملهم على ذلك، فكانت الآية مُبينة في حقهم لا مُجْمَلة. وأما عَدِيّ بن حاتم فكان بَدَوِيّاً مُشتغلاً بالصيد، ولم يكن فيه حُنْكَة أهل الحاضرة، فحمل الآية على ظاهرها ولذلك قال له رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم: «إنك لَعِرَيضُ القَفا» . فنزلت الآية تُبين لعدي مُرادَ الله عند الحاجة إلى البيان. مع أن السيوطي ذكر في التوشيح خلاف هذا ونصه:\nقال بعضهم: كأنَّ عديّاً لم يسمع هذه اللفظة من الآية لأنها نزلت قبل إسلامه بمدة، وذلك أن إسلامه كان في السنة التاسعة أو العاشرة، بعد نزول الآية بمدة، قال: علَّمنِي رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم الصلاة والصيام، فقال: «صلّ كذا، وصم كذا، فإن غابت الشمس فَكُلْ حتى يتبين لك الخيط الأبيض من الخيط الأسود، فأخذ الخيطين ... » .\nالحديث. فقال له- عليه الصلاة والسلام: «ألم أقل لك من الفجر؟» فتبين أن قوله في الحديث: «فأنزل الله مِنْ الفجر» من تصرُّف الرواة. هـ. مختصراً، فهذا صريح في أن الآية نزلت بتمامها مبينة فلم يكن فيها تأخير، والله تعالى أعلم.\nثم بيَّن الحق تعالى غاية الصوم، فقال: ثُمَّ أَتِمُّوا الصِّيامَ إِلَى اللَّيْلِ فمن أفطر مع الشك في الغروب، فعليه الكفارة، بخلاف الشك في الفجر للاستصحاب. ولما كان الاعتكاف من لوازم الصوم ذكر بعض أحكامه بإثره فقال: وَلا تُبَاشِرُوهُنَّ أي: النساء وَأَنْتُمْ عاكِفُونَ فِي الْمَساجِدِ، فالمباشرة للمعتكف حرام، وتُفسد الاعتكاف. كانت المباشرة في المسجد أو خارجَه. وكان الرجل يكون معتكفاً فيخرج فيصيب زوجَه ثم يرجع، فنزلت الآية- تِلْكَ حُدُودُ اللَّهِ قد حدها لكم، فَلا تَقْرَبُوها فضلاً عن أن تعتدوها، كَذلِكَ أي: مثل هذا البيان التام، يُبَيِّنُ اللَّهُ آياتِهِ لِلنَّاسِ لَعَلَّهُمْ يَتَّقُونَ محارمه.\nالإشارة: قد تقدم أن صوم الخواص، وخواص الخواص، هو الإمساك عن الفُضول، وعن كل ما يقطع عن الوصول. أو الإمساك عن شهود الأغيار، وعن كل ما يوجب الأكدار. فإن عزَمَت النفس على هذا الصوم وعقَدَت النيّة عليه، حلَّ لها أن تُباشر أَبْكارَ العلوم اللدنية الوهبية، والحقائق العرفانية، وتفضي إلى ثَيبات العلوم الرَّسْمية الكسبية. العلوم اللدنية الوهبية شِعارُها، والعلومُ الرسمية دِثارها . العلوم اللدنية لباس باطنها، والعلوم الرسمية لباس ظاهرها.\nقال أبو سليمان الداراني: إذا اعتادت النفوس على ترك الآثام جالت في الملكوت، ثم عادت إلى صاحبها بطرائف العلوم، من غير أن يؤدي إليها عالمٌ عِلْماً. هـ.\nقال الحقّ تعالى: عَلِمَ اللَّهُ أَنَّكُمْ كُنْتُمْ تَخْتانُونَ أَنْفُسَكُمْ بدنس الهفوات، فمنعكم من مباشرة تلك العلوم الوهبيات، فلما عقدْتُم التوبة، وعزمتم على تركها، تاب عليكم وعفا عنكم، فالآن باشروها، وابتغوا ما كتب الله لكم، من الوصول إلى معرفته، والعكوف في حضرة قُدسه، وكلوا من ثمرات تلك العلوم، واشربوا من خمرة الحيّ القيّوم، حتى يَطْلُعَ عليكم فجرُ الكشف والبيان، وتُشرق على قلوبكم شمسُ نهار العرفان، فحينئذٍ تَضْمَحِلُّ تلك العلوم، وتمحى تلك المعالم والرسوم. ولم يبقَ إلا الاستغراق في مشاهدة الحيّ القيّوم، فلا تباشروها وأنتم عاكفون في تلك المساجد. فمشاهدةُ وجهِ الحبيب تُغنْي عن مطالعة المعالم والمشاهد. تلك حدود الله فلا تقربوها، أي: لا تقفوا مع تلك العلوم وحلاوة تلك الرسوم فإنها تمنعكم من مشاهدة الحىّ القيوم. كذلك يُبين الله آياته الموضَّحة لطريق وصوله للناس، لعلهم يتقون مشاهدة ما سواه. والله تعالى أعلم.\nولما أراد الحقّ أن يتكلم على الحج قدَّم الكلام على الأموال لأنها سببٌ في وجوبه، والوصول إليه في الغالب، فقال:","quran_ar":"أُحِلَّ لَكُمْ لَيْلَةَ الصِّيامِ الرَّفَثُ إِلى نِسائِكُمْ هُنَّ لِباسٌ لَكُمْ وَأَنْتُمْ لِباسٌ لَهُنَّ عَلِمَ اللَّهُ أَنَّكُمْ كُنْتُمْ تَخْتانُونَ أَنْفُسَكُمْ فَتابَ عَلَيْكُمْ وَعَفا عَنْكُمْ فَالْآنَ بَاشِرُوهُنَّ وَابْتَغُوا ما كَتَبَ اللَّهُ لَكُمْ وَكُلُوا وَاشْرَبُوا حَتَّى يَتَبَيَّنَ لَكُمُ الْخَيْطُ الْأَبْيَضُ مِنَ الْخَيْطِ الْأَسْوَدِ مِنَ الْفَجْرِ ثُمَّ أَتِمُّوا الصِّيامَ إِلَى اللَّيْلِ وَلا تُبَاشِرُوهُنَّ وَأَنْتُمْ عاكِفُونَ فِي الْمَساجِدِ تِلْكَ حُدُودُ اللَّهِ فَلا تَقْرَبُوها كَذلِكَ يُبَيِّنُ اللَّهُ آياتِهِ لِلنَّاسِ لَعَلَّهُمْ يَتَّقُونَ (187)","quran_en":"187. Intimacy with your wives is permitted to you on the nights of fasting. They are a garment for you, and you a garment for them. God knew that you were betraying yourselves, so He turned towards you and pardoned you. Now approach them and seek what God has written for you. Eat and drink until the white thread of dawn becomes distinct to you from the black thread; then complete the fast until night. Do not approach them while you are in retreat in the mosques. These are God’s limits: do not approach them. Thus God makes His signs clear to people, so that they may be God-fearing.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"Rafath and intimate approach","html":"<p>I say: <i>rafath<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الرَّفَث<\/bdi>), pronounced with consecutive fatḥas, means intercourse and indecent speech, like <i>rufūth<\/i>, and talk with women about intercourse, as stated in the <i>Qāmūs<\/i>. The lexicographer al-Azharī calls it a comprehensive term for everything a man desires of his wife. Here it includes the meaning of intimate approach (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الإفضاء<\/bdi>), and therefore takes the preposition <i>ilā<\/i>.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-126\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B121]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: rafath ( الرَّفَث ), pronounced with consecutive fatḥas, means intercourse and indecent speech, like rufūth , and talk with women about intercourse, as stated in the Qāmūs . The lexicographer al-Azharī calls it a comprehensive term for everything a man desires of his wife. Here it includes the meaning of intimate approach ( الإفضاء ), and therefore takes the preposition ilā . [B121]"},{"type":"exposition","title":"A concession after an earlier prohibition","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, speaks in abrogation of the initial prohibition of intercourse during Ramadan after the night prayer or sleep. ʿUmar had relations with his wife after the night prayer, regretted it and came to the Messenger apologetically. Other men rose and admitted what they had done after that prayer. Then came the revelation: on the nights of fasting before dawn, approaching your wives in intercourse is permitted. The expression <i>rafath<\/i> marks the ugliness of their earlier act.<\/p><p>He explains the permission: “They are a garment for you, and you a garment for them.” I permitted intercourse because you have little patience apart from them: you embrace one another, each enveloping the other as clothing envelops its wearer. The poet says:<\/p><blockquote class=\"poetry\">When the bedfellow bends her side towards him,<br>she bends and becomes his garment.<\/blockquote><p>In that condition patience in abstaining is slight. God knew you betrayed yourselves, exposing yourselves to punishment and depriving yourselves of reward. When you repented and acknowledged your acts, He turned to you and pardoned, effacing your sins. Now approach them. <i>Mubāshara<\/i> literally brings skin against skin and is a euphemism (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">كناية<\/bdi>) for intercourse. Seek what God has written for you<\/p><p>of offspring: do not approach merely to satisfy appetite, but seek the child God has decreed and recorded on the Preserved Tablet. This, not merely satisfying desire, is the intention behind instituting marriage and creating appetite. A report says: “When the servant dies, his action ceases except for three: continuing charity, knowledge spread in people’s breasts and a righteous child praying for him.”<\/p><p>In a lengthy report from ʿĀʾisha concerning al-Ḥawlāʾ<a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/CP16\" title=\"Cairo print critical note CP16\">[CP16]<\/a>, a woman of the Helpers, the Messenger tells her: “Whenever a woman conceives by her husband, from conception she has the reward of one standing in prayer at night, fasting by day and fighting in God’s way. Every labour pang brings the reward of freeing a person, and every nursing that of freeing a slave. When she weans her child, a caller from heaven says: your previous deeds have been taken care of; begin anew for what remains.” ʿĀʾisha said: “Women have been given much good; what is there for you men?” The Prophet laughed and said: “Whenever a believing man takes his wife’s hand to court her, God writes him a good deed; embracing her brings ten; lying with her twenty; and intercourse is better than the world and all it contains. When he rises to bathe, water passes over no hair on his body without a sin being effaced and a degree bestowed. What he receives for bathing is better than the world and all it contains. God glories before the angels: ‘Look at My servant, rising on a cold night to bathe from major ritual impurity, certain that I am his Lord. Bear witness that I have forgiven him.’” From al-Thaʿlabī.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-127\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B122]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, speaks in abrogation of the initial prohibition of intercourse during Ramadan after the night prayer or sleep. ʿUmar had relations with his wife after the night prayer, regretted it and came to the Messenger apologetically. Other men rose and admitted what they had done after that prayer. Then came the revelation: on the nights of fasting before dawn, approaching your wives in intercourse is permitted. The expression rafath marks the ugliness of their earlier act. He explains the permission: “They are a garment for you, and you a garment for them.” I permitted intercourse because you have little patience apart from them: you embrace one another, each enveloping the other as clothing envelops its wearer. The poet says: When the bedfellow bends her side towards him, she bends and becomes his garment. In that condition patience in abstaining is slight. God knew you betrayed yourselves, exposing yourselves to punishment and depriving yourselves of reward. When you repented and acknowledged your acts, He turned to you and pardoned, effacing your sins. Now approach them. Mubāshara literally brings skin against skin and is a euphemism ( كناية ) for intercourse. Seek what God has written for you of offspring: do not approach merely to satisfy appetite, but seek the child God has decreed and recorded on the Preserved Tablet. This, not merely satisfying desire, is the intention behind instituting marriage and creating appetite. A report says: “When the servant dies, his action ceases except for three: continuing charity, knowledge spread in people’s breasts and a righteous child praying for him.” In a lengthy report from ʿĀʾisha concerning al-Ḥawlāʾ [CP16] , a woman of the Helpers, the Messenger tells her: “Whenever a woman conceives by her husband, from conception she has the reward of one standing in prayer at night, fasting by day and fighting in God’s way. Every labour pang brings the reward of freeing a person, and every nursing that of freeing a slave. When she weans her child, a caller from heaven says: your previous deeds have been taken care of; begin anew for what remains.” ʿĀʾisha said: “Women have been given much good; what is there for you men?” The Prophet laughed and said: “Whenever a believing man takes his wife’s hand to court her, God writes him a good deed; embracing her brings ten; lying with her twenty; and intercourse is better than the world and all it contains. When he rises to bathe, water passes over no hair on his body without a sin being effaced and a degree bestowed. What he receives for bathing is better than the world and all it contains. God glories before the angels: ‘Look at My servant, rising on a cold night to bathe from major ritual impurity, certain that I am his Lord. Bear witness that I have forgiven him.’” From al-Thaʿlabī. [B122]"},{"type":"exposition","title":"The two threads and the timing of clarification","html":"<p>The Real permits eating and drinking on the fasting night until dawn: “Eat and drink until the white thread becomes distinct to you from the black thread.” The first light of dawn appearing horizontally across the horizon is likened to a white thread, and the darkness of night stretching beside it to a black thread.<\/p><p>“Of dawn” was not revealed until some time later. Some Companions took the words literally, putting a white and black thread beneath their pillow and continuing to eat while looking at them, unable to distinguish them. Among them was ʿAdī ibn Ḥātim. He said: “I went early to the Messenger and told him. He laughed and said, ‘You are indeed broad in the nape! It means the whiteness of day and the blackness of night.’” The report is established in al-Bukhārī and elsewhere. Al-Zamakhsharī objected that this postpones clarification beyond the time of need (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">تأخير البيان عن وقت الحاجة<\/bdi>), which is impermissible because it imposes what cannot be performed.<\/p><p>The answer given is that clarification was delayed until, not beyond, the time of need, which is permissible. When the two-thread passage was revealed, the Messenger and believers understood God’s intention and acted accordingly. For them it was clear, not indeterminate (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مجملة<\/bdi>). ʿAdī, a Bedouin occupied with hunting and lacking settled people’s experience, took it literally; hence “broad in the nape.” The verse then clarified God’s intention for him when clarification became necessary.<\/p><p>Yet al-Suyūṭī states otherwise in the <i>Tawshīḥ<\/i>: someone said ʿAdī apparently had not heard this part of the verse, which descended long before his conversion in year nine or ten. He said: “The Messenger taught me prayer and fasting: ‘Pray thus and fast thus; when the sun sets, eat until the white thread becomes distinct from the black.’ I took the two threads …” The Prophet asked him, “Did I not say, ‘of dawn’?” This shows that “then God revealed ‘of dawn’” in the report results from the narrators’ handling of it. Here ends the abridged quotation.<a class=\"note-ref\" href=\"#/note/XVI-N002\">[XVI-N002]<\/a> This explicitly makes the verse clear and complete upon revelation, with no delay. And God Most High knows best.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-128\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B123]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real permits eating and drinking on the fasting night until dawn: “Eat and drink until the white thread becomes distinct to you from the black thread.” The first light of dawn appearing horizontally across the horizon is likened to a white thread, and the darkness of night stretching beside it to a black thread. “Of dawn” was not revealed until some time later. Some Companions took the words literally, putting a white and black thread beneath their pillow and continuing to eat while looking at them, unable to distinguish them. Among them was ʿAdī ibn Ḥātim. He said: “I went early to the Messenger and told him. He laughed and said, ‘You are indeed broad in the nape! It means the whiteness of day and the blackness of night.’” The report is established in al-Bukhārī and elsewhere. Al-Zamakhsharī objected that this postpones clarification beyond the time of need ( تأخير البيان عن وقت الحاجة ), which is impermissible because it imposes what cannot be performed. The answer given is that clarification was delayed until, not beyond, the time of need, which is permissible. When the two-thread passage was revealed, the Messenger and believers understood God’s intention and acted accordingly. For them it was clear, not indeterminate ( مجملة ). ʿAdī, a Bedouin occupied with hunting and lacking settled people’s experience, took it literally; hence “broad in the nape.” The verse then clarified God’s intention for him when clarification became necessary. Yet al-Suyūṭī states otherwise in the Tawshīḥ : someone said ʿAdī apparently had not heard this part of the verse, which descended long before his conversion in year nine or ten. He said: “The Messenger taught me prayer and fasting: ‘Pray thus and fast thus; when the sun sets, eat until the white thread becomes distinct from the black.’ I took the two threads …” The Prophet asked him, “Did I not say, ‘of dawn’?” This shows that “then God revealed ‘of dawn’” in the report results from the narrators’ handling of it. Here ends the abridged quotation. [XVI-N002] This explicitly makes the verse clear and complete upon revelation, with no delay. And God Most High knows best. [B123]"},{"type":"exposition","title":"The end of fasting and intercourse during retreat","html":"<p>The Real specifies the fast’s limit: “Then complete the fast until night.” Whoever breaks it while doubtful whether sunset has occurred owes expiation, unlike doubt about dawn, because the preceding state is presumed to continue (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الاستصحاب<\/bdi>).<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-129\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B124]<\/a><\/p><p>Since retreat (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الاعتكاف<\/bdi>) is connected with fasting, He follows with part of its rulings: do not have relations with women while in retreat in the mosques. Intercourse is forbidden to one in retreat and invalidates it, whether inside or outside the mosque. A man would go out from retreat, have relations with his wife and return; the verse was revealed. These are limits God has set for you: do not approach them, much less cross them. With such complete explanation God clarifies His signs, so that people avoid His prohibitions.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real specifies the fast’s limit: “Then complete the fast until night.” Whoever breaks it while doubtful whether sunset has occurred owes expiation, unlike doubt about dawn, because the preceding state is presumed to continue ( الاستصحاب ). [B124] Since retreat ( الاعتكاف ) is connected with fasting, He follows with part of its rulings: do not have relations with women while in retreat in the mosques. Intercourse is forbidden to one in retreat and invalidates it, whether inside or outside the mosque. A man would go out from retreat, have relations with his wife and return; the verse was revealed. These are limits God has set for you: do not approach them, much less cross them. With such complete explanation God clarifies His signs, so that people avoid His prohibitions."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Gifted knowledge, acquired learning and witnessing beyond both","html":"<p>The fast of the elect and elect of the elect has been explained: abstaining from superfluities and whatever prevents arrival, or from witnessing others and whatever produces turbidity. When the soul resolves upon this fast and forms its intention, it may approach the virgin, divinely bestowed sciences and gnostic realities, and the previously possessed formal, acquired sciences. Gifted knowledge (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">العلوم اللدنية الوهبية<\/bdi>) is its inner garment; formal knowledge its outer cloak. The first clothes its inward life, the second its outward life.<\/p><p>Abū Sulaymān al-Dārānī said: “When souls become accustomed to abandoning sins, they range through the dominion and return to their possessor with wondrous knowledge, without a scholar conveying knowledge to them.”<\/p><p>The Real says: God knew that you betrayed yourselves through the pollution of lapses and prevented your approaching these gifted sciences. Once you resolved upon repentance and abandoning the lapses, He accepted and pardoned you. Now approach the sciences, seeking the arrival at His knowledge and abiding in His holy presence that He wrote for you. Eat their fruits and drink the wine of the Living, Self-Subsisting until unveiling’s dawn rises and the day of gnosis shines upon your hearts.<\/p><p>Then those sciences disappear and their landmarks and traces are effaced; nothing remains but absorption in witnessing the Living, Self-Subsisting. Do not approach them while abiding in those mosques. Seeing the Beloved’s Face dispenses with contemplating landmarks and scenes. These are God’s limits: do not stop with those sciences and the sweetness of their traces, for they prevent witnessing the Living, Self-Subsisting. Thus God clarifies His signs showing people the path of arrival, so that they guard against witnessing other than He. And God Most High knows best.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"The fast of the elect and elect of the elect has been explained: abstaining from superfluities and whatever prevents arrival, or from witnessing others and whatever produces turbidity. When the soul resolves upon this fast and forms its intention, it may approach the virgin, divinely bestowed sciences and gnostic realities, and the previously possessed formal, acquired sciences. Gifted knowledge ( العلوم اللدنية الوهبية ) is its inner garment; formal knowledge its outer cloak. The first clothes its inward life, the second its outward life. Abū Sulaymān al-Dārānī said: “When souls become accustomed to abandoning sins, they range through the dominion and return to their possessor with wondrous knowledge, without a scholar conveying knowledge to them.” The Real says: God knew that you betrayed yourselves through the pollution of lapses and prevented your approaching these gifted sciences. Once you resolved upon repentance and abandoning the lapses, He accepted and pardoned you. Now approach the sciences, seeking the arrival at His knowledge and abiding in His holy presence that He wrote for you. Eat their fruits and drink the wine of the Living, Self-Subsisting until unveiling’s dawn rises and the day of gnosis shines upon your hearts. Then those sciences disappear and their landmarks and traces are effaced; nothing remains but absorption in witnessing the Living, Self-Subsisting. Do not approach them while abiding in those mosques. Seeing the Beloved’s Face dispenses with contemplating landmarks and scenes. These are God’s limits: do not stop with those sciences and the sweetness of their traces, for they prevent witnessing the Living, Self-Subsisting. Thus God clarifies His signs showing people the path of arrival, so that they guard against witnessing other than He. And God Most High knows best."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>Before speaking of pilgrimage, the Real mentions wealth, generally a condition of its obligation and of reaching it:<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"Before speaking of pilgrimage, the Real mentions wealth, generally a condition of its obligation and of reaching it:"}],"cairo_notes":[{"id":"C215.1","page":215,"ar":"وهو النابغة الجعدي.","en":"He is al-Nābigha al-Jaʿdī.","anchor":"إذا ما الضجيع ثنى عطفها"},{"id":"C216.1","page":216,"ar":"الحديث موضوع، ذكره ابن الجوزي في الموضوعات وابن عراق في تنزيه الشريعة. قال الحافظ ابن حجر في الإصابة: سند هذا الحديث واه جدا. وقال الدارقطني: هذا حديث باطل، وقال: ذهب عبد الرحمن بن مهدي وأبو داود إلى زياد بن ميمون - أحد رجال سند هذا الحديث - فأنكرا عليه هذا الحديث، فقال: اشهدوا أني قد رجعت عنه.","en":"This hadith is fabricated. Ibn al-Jawzī mentions it in al-Mawḍūʿāt, and Ibn ʿIrāq in Tanzīh al-sharīʿa. The hadith master Ibn Ḥajar says in al-Iṣāba that its chain of transmission is extremely weak. Al-Dāraquṭnī says, “This is an invalid hadith.” He also relates that ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Mahdī and Abū Dāwūd went to Ziyād ibn Maymūn—one of the men in its chain—and challenged him over this hadith. He said, “Bear witness that I have retracted it.”","anchor":"في قصة الحولاء ... ما من امرأة حملت من زوجها"},{"id":"C217.1","page":217,"ar":"الشعار: ما ولي جسد الإنسان دون ما سواه من الثياب، والدثار: الثوب الذي يكون فوق الشعار.","en":"Shiʿār is the garment next to a person’s body, beneath the other clothes. Dithār is the garment worn over the shiʿār.","anchor":"العلوم اللدنية الوهبية شعارها، والعلوم الرسمية دثارها"}],"english_characters":8115,"arabic_characters":7042},{"id":"q2-188","q":"2:188","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:188 · Unjust wealth, judicial judgement and the hand of the Giver","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 218–219","cairo":"I:218–219","source_page_first":218,"source_page_last":219,"ar":"وَلا تَأْكُلُوا أَمْوالَكُمْ بَيْنَكُمْ بِالْباطِلِ وَتُدْلُوا بِها إِلَى الْحُكَّامِ لِتَأْكُلُوا فَرِيقاً مِنْ أَمْوالِ النَّاسِ بِالْإِثْمِ وَأَنْتُمْ تَعْلَمُونَ (188)\nقلت: أصل الإدلاء: إرسالُ الدَّلْو في الماء ليتوصل به إلى أخذ الماء من البئر، ثم أُطلق في كل ما يتوسل به إلى شيء، يقال: أدلَى بمالِه إلى الحكام، أي: دفعه رِشْوة، ليتوصل بذلك إلى أخذ أكثر منه، وهو المراد هنا، وفي القاموس: أدلى برَحمِه: توسَّل، وبحُجَّتِه: أحضرَها، وإليه بمالهِ: دفَعه. ومنه: (وتُدْلوا بها إلى الحكام) . هـ. و (تدلوا) معطوف على (تأكلوا) ، مَنْهِي عنهما معاً.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: وَلا تَأْكُلُوا يا معشر المسلمين أَمْوالَكُمْ أي: أموال بعضكم بعضاً، بِالْباطِلِ أي: بغير حقّ شرعي إما بغير حق أصلا كالغصب والسرقة والخيانة والخَدْع والتطفيف والغش وغير ذلك. أو بحق باطل كما يؤخذ في السحر والكهانة والفأل والقِمار والجاه، وهَدِية المِدْيان ، وهدية القرض، والضمان،\nوالرشوة، والربا، وغير ذلك مما نهى الشارعُ عنه. ولا يدخل في ذلك التمائم والعزائم إذا كان بالقرآن أو السنّة وغلَب الشفاء، وكذلك لا يدخل أيضاً الغَبْن، إذا كان البائعُ عالماً بالمَبِيع.\nأو وَلا تَأْكُلُوا أَمْوالَكُمْ بَيْنَكُمْ بِالْباطِلِ بأن تُنفقوها في المَلاهي والزنا والشرب واللواط، وغير ذلك من المحرمات، ولا تُدْلُوا أي: تتوسلوا بها، أي: بدفعها إِلَى الْحُكَّامِ رشوة لِتَأْكُلُوا فَرِيقاً مِنْ أَمْوالِ النَّاسِ بأن يحكم لكم بها القاضي، تأخذونها متلبسين بِالْإِثْمِ أي: بالمعصية وَأَنْتُمْ تَعْلَمُونَ أنها لغيركم فإنَّ حُكْمَ الحاكِم لا يُحِلُّ حراماً.\nوفي الحديث عنه صلّى الله عليه وسلم قال: «إنَّما أنا بَشرٌ مثلُكُم، ولعلَّ بعضَكُمْ أن يكون الْحَنَ بحُجَّتِهِ مِنْ بَعضٍ فأقضي لهُ، فمن قَضَيتُ له بشيء من مال أخيه فإنَما أقَطَعُ له قطعةً من النارِ» .\nالإشارة: الباطلُ كلُّ ما سوى الحقّ، فكل من كان يأخذ من يد الخلق ولا يشاهد فيهم الحقّ فإنما يأخذ أموال الناس بالباطل. قال في الحكم: «لا تمدن يديك إلى الأخذ من الخلائق إلا أن ترى أن المعطي فيهم مولاك، فإذا كنت كذلك فخذ ما وافقك العلم» . ويحتاج العامل بهذا إلى عَسةٍ كبيرةٍ، وشُهودٍ قَوِي، حتى يَفْنَى عَنْ نظرهِ مشاهدةُ الخلق في شهود الملك الحق. وكان بعضهم يطلب مَنْ هذا وصْفُه فيعطي للفقير العطاء، ويقول: خذ، لا لك، فلا يسمع من أحد شيئاً، حتى أعطَى لبعض الفقراء، وقال: خذ، لا لك، فقال: أقبضُ لا منك. هـ. قلت: الوصول إلى الحكام على شأن الدنيا أو للانتصار للنفس حرام في طريق الخصوص، بل يصبر حتى يحكم الله بينه وبين خصمه، وهو خير الحاكمين، فإن اضطُرَّ إلى شيء ولم يجد بُدّاً منه فليُوكِّلْ، وبالله التوفيق.\nوَلَمَّا أراد الحقُّ تعالى أن يتكلم على أحكام الحج، قدَّم الكلام على الهلال لأنه معتبر فى الحج، أداء وقضاء، فقال:","quran_ar":"وَلا تَأْكُلُوا أَمْوالَكُمْ بَيْنَكُمْ بِالْباطِلِ وَتُدْلُوا بِها إِلَى الْحُكَّامِ لِتَأْكُلُوا فَرِيقاً مِنْ أَمْوالِ النَّاسِ بِالْإِثْمِ وَأَنْتُمْ تَعْلَمُونَ (188)","quran_en":"188. Do not consume one another’s wealth falsely, nor offer it to judges in order knowingly to consume a portion of people’s wealth sinfully.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"Lowering a bucket and presenting a bribe","html":"<p>I say: <i>idlāʾ<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الإدلاء<\/bdi>) originally means lowering a bucket into water to draw from a well, then anything used to attain something. “He offered his wealth to the judges” means paying a bribe to obtain more; that is intended here. The <i>Qāmūs<\/i> gives: he invoked his kinship as a means; presented his proof; and offered his wealth to someone, meaning handed it over. Hence “offer it to judges.” The verb is coordinated with “consume,” and both are prohibited.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: idlāʾ ( الإدلاء ) originally means lowering a bucket into water to draw from a well, then anything used to attain something. “He offered his wealth to the judges” means paying a bribe to obtain more; that is intended here. The Qāmūs gives: he invoked his kinship as a means; presented his proof; and offered his wealth to someone, meaning handed it over. Hence “offer it to judges.” The verb is coordinated with “consume,” and both are prohibited."},{"type":"exposition","title":"A court’s decision does not make the forbidden lawful","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: Muslims, do not consume one another’s wealth falsely, without a valid right under the Sacred Law. This may mean no right at all—usurpation, theft, betrayal, deception, short measure, fraud and the like—or a false claim to a right, as with payments for sorcery, soothsaying, omens, gambling, influence, a debtor’s gift, gifts connected with loans, guarantees,<\/p><p>bribes, usury and other things forbidden by the Lawgiver. This does not include amulets and invocations based upon Qurʾān or Sunna when healing is predominant, nor disproportion in an exchange when the seller knows the thing sold.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-130\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B125]<\/a><\/p><p>Or: do not consume wealth falsely by spending it on diversions, illicit intercourse, drinking, sodomy and other prohibitions. Do not use it as a means by paying bribes to judges, so that a judge awards you a portion of others’ property which you take sinfully while knowing it belongs to another. A judge’s judgement does not make an unlawful thing lawful.<\/p><p>The Prophet said: “I am only a human being like you. Perhaps one of you presents his argument more eloquently than another and I judge in his favour. Whoever I award something of his brother’s wealth, I award him only a piece of the Fire.”<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-131\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B126]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: Muslims, do not consume one another’s wealth falsely, without a valid right under the Sacred Law. This may mean no right at all—usurpation, theft, betrayal, deception, short measure, fraud and the like—or a false claim to a right, as with payments for sorcery, soothsaying, omens, gambling, influence, a debtor’s gift, gifts connected with loans, guarantees, bribes, usury and other things forbidden by the Lawgiver. This does not include amulets and invocations based upon Qurʾān or Sunna when healing is predominant, nor disproportion in an exchange when the seller knows the thing sold. [B125] Or: do not consume wealth falsely by spending it on diversions, illicit intercourse, drinking, sodomy and other prohibitions. Do not use it as a means by paying bribes to judges, so that a judge awards you a portion of others’ property which you take sinfully while knowing it belongs to another. A judge’s judgement does not make an unlawful thing lawful. The Prophet said: “I am only a human being like you. Perhaps one of you presents his argument more eloquently than another and I judge in his favour. Whoever I award something of his brother’s wealth, I award him only a piece of the Fire.” [B126]"},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Receiving through creation without forgetting God","html":"<p>Falsehood is everything besides the Real. Whoever receives from creation’s hand without witnessing the Real in them takes people’s wealth falsely. The <i>Ḥikam<\/i> says: “Do not stretch your hands to receive from creatures unless you see that the Giver through them is your Master. When you are thus, take what knowledge permits you.” Acting upon this requires great vigilance and strong witnessing, until the sight of creation disappears from one’s gaze in witnessing the True King.<\/p><p>Someone sought a person of this quality. Giving to a poor man, he would say, “Take—not for you,” but heard no answer. Finally he gave to one of the poor and said, “Take—not for you.” The man replied, “I receive—not from you.”<\/p><p>I say: approaching rulers over worldly matters or to vindicate oneself is forbidden in the path of the elect. Rather, one waits patiently until God judges between oneself and one’s opponent; He is the best of judges. If compelled by an unavoidable need, one should appoint an agent. Through God is success.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-132\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B127]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"Falsehood is everything besides the Real. Whoever receives from creation’s hand without witnessing the Real in them takes people’s wealth falsely. The Ḥikam says: “Do not stretch your hands to receive from creatures unless you see that the Giver through them is your Master. When you are thus, take what knowledge permits you.” Acting upon this requires great vigilance and strong witnessing, until the sight of creation disappears from one’s gaze in witnessing the True King. Someone sought a person of this quality. Giving to a poor man, he would say, “Take—not for you,” but heard no answer. Finally he gave to one of the poor and said, “Take—not for you.” The man replied, “I receive—not from you.” I say: approaching rulers over worldly matters or to vindicate oneself is forbidden in the path of the elect. Rather, one waits patiently until God judges between oneself and one’s opponent; He is the best of judges. If compelled by an unavoidable need, one should appoint an agent. Through God is success. [B127]"},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>Before the rulings of pilgrimage, the Real speaks of the crescent, considered in both performing and making up pilgrimage:<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"Before the rulings of pilgrimage, the Real speaks of the crescent, considered in both performing and making up pilgrimage:"}],"cairo_notes":[{"id":"C218.1","page":218,"ar":"رجل مديان: إذا كان عادته أن يأخذ بالدين.","en":"A man is called midyān when he habitually incurs debt.","anchor":"وهدية المديان"},{"id":"C219.1","page":219,"ar":"أي: اجتهاد وجد، من عس يعس: إذا طلب.","en":"That is, exertion and earnest effort, from ʿassa yaʿussu, “to seek.”","anchor":"إلى عسة كبيرة"}],"english_characters":2833,"arabic_characters":2421},{"id":"q2-189","q":"2:189","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:189 · The crescents and entering each house by its door","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 219–222","cairo":"I:219–222","source_page_first":219,"source_page_last":222,"ar":"يَسْئَلُونَكَ عَنِ الْأَهِلَّةِ قُلْ هِيَ مَواقِيتُ لِلنَّاسِ وَالْحَجِّ ...\nقلت: الذي سأله معاذُ بن جبل وثعلبةُ بن غنمة ، فقالا: يا رسول الله: ما بال الهلال يبدو رقيقاً كالخيط، ثم لا يزال يزيد حتى يستوي، ثم لا يزال ينقص حتى يرجع كالخيط؟ فقال الحق جل جلاله: يَسْئَلُونَكَ عَنِ الْأَهِلَّةِ\nأي: عن حكمة اختلاف الأهلة بالزيادة والنقص، قُلْ لهم يا محمد: هِيَ مَواقِيتُ لِلنَّاسِ يُوقِّتُون بها دُيونهم، ويعرفون بها أوقات زَرْعهم، وعِدَدَ نسائهم وصيامهم. وهي أيضاً مواقيتُ للحج، يعرفون بذلك وقت دخوله وخروجه، فيعرفون الأداء من القضاء، فلو كانت على حالة واحدة لم يعرفوا ذلك. أجابهم الحق تعالى بغير ما ينتظرون إشارة إلى أن السؤال عن سر الاختلاف، ليس فيه منفعة شرعية، وإنما ينبغي الاهتمام بما فيه منفعة دينية.\nقال أهل الهيئة: إن نوره من نُور الشمس، وجِرْمه أَطْلَس، فكلما بعد من مُسَامتة الشمس قابَله نورُها، فإذا قرب منها لم يقابله من نورها إلا بعض جِرمه، فإذا دخل تحتها في الفلك كان ظَهره كله إليها، فلم يقابله شيء من نورها، فإذا خرج من تحتِها قابله بقدر ذلك، والله تعالى أعلم.\nالإشارة: إذا ظهر هلال السعادة في أفق الإرادة، وهبَّت ريح الهداية من ناحية سابق العناية، دخل وقت حج القلوب إلى حضرة علام الغيوب، فهلال الهداية للسائرين، وهم أرباب الأحوال أهل التلوين، يزداد نوره بزيادة اليقين، وينقص بنقصانه، على حسب ضعف حاله وقوته، حتى يتحقق الوصال، ويُرزق صفة الكمال. وأنشدوا:\nكُلَّ يومٍ تَتَلَوَّنْ ... غيرُ هذا بِكَ أَجْمَلْ\nفصاحب التلوين بين الزيادة والنقصان، إلى أن تطلع عليه شمس العرفان، فإذا طلعت شمس العرفان فليس بعدها زيادة ولا نقصان، وأنشدوا:\nطَلَعَتْ شمسُ مَنْ أُحِبُّ بلَيْلٍ ... واستضاءتْ فَمَا تَلاَهَا غُرُوبُ\nإنَّ شَمْسَ النَّهارِ تَغْربُ باللي ... ل وشَمْسَ القُلوب لَيْسَتْ تَغيبُ\nبخلاف صاحب التمكين فإنه أبداً في ضياء معرفته، متمكن في بُرج سعادته، لا يلحق شمسَه كسوف ولا حجابٌ، ولا يستر نورَها ظلمةٌ ولا سحاب، فلو طلب الحجاب لم يُجَبْ. قال بعض العارفين: (لو كُلفت أن أرى غيره لم أستطع، فإنه لا غير معه حتى أشهده) .\nثم حذَّر الحق تعالى مما ابتدعه المشركون في الحج، فقال:\n... وَلَيْسَ الْبِرُّ بِأَنْ تَأْتُوا الْبُيُوتَ مِنْ ظُهُورِها وَلكِنِ الْبِرُّ مَنِ اتَّقى وَأْتُوا الْبُيُوتَ مِنْ أَبْوابِها وَاتَّقُوا اللَّهَ لَعَلَّكُمْ تُفْلِحُونَ (189)\nقلت: كانت الأنصار إذا حَجُّوا أو اعتمروا، يقولون: لا يَحُول بيننا وبين السماء سَقْفٌ، حتى يدخلوا بيوتهم، فإذا رجعوا تسوَّرُوا الجُدْران، أو نَقَبُوا في ظهور بيوتهم، فجاء رجل منهم فدخل من الباب، فَعُيِّر بذلك، فأنزل الحقّ جلّ جلاله: وَلَيْسَ الْبِرُّ أي: الطاعة، بِأَنْ تَأْتُوا الْبُيُوتَ مِنْ ظُهُورِها فتتسوروها، أو تنقُبوا من أعلاها، وَلكِنَّ الْبِرَّ مَنِ اتَّقى المحارم وخالف الشهوات.\nأو: ليس البر بأن تعكسوا مسائلكم بأن تسألوا عما لا نفع لكم فيه، وتتركوا مسائل العلم التي تنفعكم في العاجل والآجل. وَلكِنَّ الْبِرَّ مَنِ اتَّقى ذلك، وَأْتُوا بيوت العلم من أبوابها، فتُحسنون السؤال وتتأدبون في المقال، وتقدمون الأهم فالأهم، والأنفع فالأنفع. وَاتَّقُوا اللَّهَ فلا تُغيروا أحكامه، ولا تعترضوا على أفعاله، لَعَلَّكُمْ تُفْلِحُونَ بتوفيقه وهدايته.\nالإشارة: اعلم أن البيوت التي يدخلها المريد ثلاثة: بيت الشريعة وبيت الطريقة وبيت الحقيقة، ولكل واحد أبواب فمن أتى البيت من بابه دخل. ومن أتاه من غيره طُرد.\nفبيت الشريعة له ثلاثة أبواب: الباب الأول: التوبة، فإذا دخل هذا الباب، وحقَّق التوبة بأركانها وشروطها، استقبله باب الاستقامة، وهي: متابعة الرسول في أقواله وأفعاله وأحواله، فإذا دخله، وحقق الاستقامة، استقبله باب التقوى بأقسامها. فإذا حقق التقوى ظاهراً وباطناً، دخل بيت الشريعة المطهرة، وتنزه في محاسنه ومعانيه، ثم يروم دخول بيت الطريقة، وله ثلاثة أبواب:\nالباب الأول: الإخلاص وهو: إفراد العمل لله من غير حرف ولا حظ، فإذا حقق الإخلاص استقبله باب التخلية وهى التطهير من العيوب الباطنة، وهي لا تنحصر، لكن من ظفر بالشيخ أطلعه عليها، وعلّمه أدويتها، فإذا حقق التخلية استقبله باب التحلية، وهي: الاتصاف بأنواع الفضائل كالصبر والحلم والصدق والطمأنينة والسخاء والإيثار، وغير ذلك من أنواع الكمالات. فإذا حقق الإخلاص والتخلية والتحلية فقد حقق بيت الطريقة، ثم يستقبله بيت الحقيقة.\nفأول ما يقرع باب المراقبة، وهي: حفظ القلب والسر من الخواطر الردية، فإذا تطهر القلب من الخواطر الساكنة، استشرف على باب المشاهدة، وهى: محو الرسوم في مشاهدة أنوار الحي القيّوم، أو تلطيف الأواني عند ظهور المعاني، فإذا دخل باب المشاهدة، وسكن فيها، استقبله باب المعرفة، وهي محلّ الرسوخ والتمكين، وهي الغاية والمنتهى، فبيت الحقيقة هو مسجد الحضرة الربانية. وما بقي بعدها إلا الترقي في المقامات، وزيادة المعارف والكشوفات أبداً سرمداً، منحنا الله من ذلك حظا وافرا بمنّه وكرمه.\nولمّا كان البيت الحرام عند فرض الحج معمورا بالكفار، أمرهم بجهادهم ليتمكن المسلمون من الحج، فقال:","quran_ar":"يَسْئَلُونَكَ عَنِ الْأَهِلَّةِ قُلْ هِيَ مَواقِيتُ لِلنَّاسِ وَالْحَجِّ وَلَيْسَ الْبِرُّ بِأَنْ تَأْتُوا الْبُيُوتَ مِنْ ظُهُورِها وَلكِنِ الْبِرُّ مَنِ اتَّقى وَأْتُوا الْبُيُوتَ مِنْ أَبْوابِها وَاتَّقُوا اللَّهَ لَعَلَّكُمْ تُفْلِحُونَ (189)","quran_en":"189. They ask you about the crescents. Say, “They mark times for people and for pilgrimage.” Righteousness is not that you enter houses from their backs; righteousness is in one who fears God. Enter houses by their doors, and fear God, so that you may prosper.","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"The useful answer to a question about the moon","html":"<p>I say: Muʿādh ibn Jabal and Thaʿlaba ibn Ghanama asked him: “Messenger of God, why does the crescent appear thin as a thread, then increase until full, then diminish until it returns to a thread?” The Real said: they ask you about the crescents,<\/p><p>about the wisdom of their increase and decrease. Tell them, Muḥammad: they mark times for people’s debts, cultivation, women’s waiting periods and fasting. They also mark pilgrimage’s times, its beginning and end, distinguishing timely performance from making it up. Were the moon always in one condition, these would not be known. The Real answers differently from what they expected, indicating that asking about the secret of the change yields no benefit under revealed law; attention should be given to what benefits religion.<\/p><p>Astronomers say that its light is from the sun and its body smooth. As it moves away from alignment with the sun, the sun’s light faces it. When near the sun, only part of its body receives that light. When it passes beneath the sun in the sphere, its whole back faces the sun and none of its [visible face] receives the light. As it emerges from beneath, it receives light to that extent. And God Most High knows best.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: Muʿādh ibn Jabal and Thaʿlaba ibn Ghanama asked him: “Messenger of God, why does the crescent appear thin as a thread, then increase until full, then diminish until it returns to a thread?” The Real said: they ask you about the crescents, about the wisdom of their increase and decrease. Tell them, Muḥammad: they mark times for people’s debts, cultivation, women’s waiting periods and fasting. They also mark pilgrimage’s times, its beginning and end, distinguishing timely performance from making it up. Were the moon always in one condition, these would not be known. The Real answers differently from what they expected, indicating that asking about the secret of the change yields no benefit under revealed law; attention should be given to what benefits religion. Astronomers say that its light is from the sun and its body smooth. As it moves away from alignment with the sun, the sun’s light faces it. When near the sun, only part of its body receives that light. When it passes beneath the sun in the sphere, its whole back faces the sun and none of its [visible face] receives the light. As it emerges from beneath, it receives light to that extent. And God Most High knows best."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"The crescent of changing states and the sun of establishment","html":"<p>When happiness’s crescent appears on the horizon of aspiration and guidance’s wind blows from preceding divine care, the time arrives for hearts to make pilgrimage to the presence of the Knower of unseen things. Guidance’s crescent belongs to travellers, possessors of states and changing coloration (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">التلوين<\/bdi>). Its light grows as certainty grows and diminishes as certainty diminishes, according to the strength or weakness of the state, until union is realized and perfection granted. They recite:<\/p><blockquote class=\"poetry\">Every day you change your colour;<br>something other than this would become you better.<\/blockquote><p>One subject to changing states passes between increase and decrease until gnosis’s sun rises. Once it rises there is no subsequent increase or decrease. They recite:<\/p><blockquote class=\"poetry\">The sun of the one I love rose by night,<br>shining with no sunset to follow.<br>The daytime sun sets in the night,<br>but the sun of hearts never disappears.<\/blockquote><p>The one established (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">صاحب التمكين<\/bdi>), by contrast, remains always in gnosis’s radiance, fixed in happiness’s constellation. Neither eclipse nor veil touches his sun; darkness and clouds do not obscure its light. Were he to ask for a veil, his request would not be granted. A gnostic said: “Were I charged to see another than He, I could not, for no other is with Him for me to witness.”<\/p><p>The Real then warns against what the associators innovated in pilgrimage:<\/p><div class=\"quranpanel embedded-quran\" data-q=\"2:189b\"><div class=\"meta\">Qurʾānic continuation · 2:189b<\/div><div class=\"quranar\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">وَلَيْسَ الْبِرُّ بِأَنْ تَأْتُوا الْبُيُوتَ مِنْ ظُهُورِهَا وَلَكِنِ الْبِرُّ مَنِ اتَّقَى وَأْتُوا الْبُيُوتَ مِنْ أَبْوَابِهَا وَاتَّقُوا اللَّهَ لَعَلَّكُمْ تُفْلِحُونَ<\/div><div class=\"quranen\">Righteousness is not that you enter houses from their backs; righteousness is in one who fears God. Enter houses by their doors, and fear God, so that you may prosper.<\/div><\/div>","authorial":true,"text":"When happiness’s crescent appears on the horizon of aspiration and guidance’s wind blows from preceding divine care, the time arrives for hearts to make pilgrimage to the presence of the Knower of unseen things. Guidance’s crescent belongs to travellers, possessors of states and changing coloration ( التلوين ). Its light grows as certainty grows and diminishes as certainty diminishes, according to the strength or weakness of the state, until union is realized and perfection granted. They recite: Every day you change your colour; something other than this would become you better. One subject to changing states passes between increase and decrease until gnosis’s sun rises. Once it rises there is no subsequent increase or decrease. They recite: The sun of the one I love rose by night, shining with no sunset to follow. The daytime sun sets in the night, but the sun of hearts never disappears. The one established ( صاحب التمكين ), by contrast, remains always in gnosis’s radiance, fixed in happiness’s constellation. Neither eclipse nor veil touches his sun; darkness and clouds do not obscure its light. Were he to ask for a veil, his request would not be granted. A gnostic said: “Were I charged to see another than He, I could not, for no other is with Him for me to witness.” The Real then warns against what the associators innovated in pilgrimage: Qurʾānic continuation · 2:189b وَلَيْسَ الْبِرُّ بِأَنْ تَأْتُوا الْبُيُوتَ مِنْ ظُهُورِهَا وَلَكِنِ الْبِرُّ مَنِ اتَّقَى وَأْتُوا الْبُيُوتَ مِنْ أَبْوَابِهَا وَاتَّقُوا اللَّهَ لَعَلَّكُمْ تُفْلِحُونَ Righteousness is not that you enter houses from their backs; righteousness is in one who fears God. Enter houses by their doors, and fear God, so that you may prosper."},{"type":"exposition","title":"Ritual custom and the courtesy of asking","html":"<p>I say: when the Helpers made the greater or lesser pilgrimage, they would say that no roof should intervene between them and heaven until they entered their houses. On returning they climbed walls or opened holes in the backs of their houses. One man entered by the door and was reproached. The Real revealed: righteousness—obedience—is not entering from behind, climbing over or opening a hole above. Righteousness belongs to one avoiding prohibitions and opposing appetites.<\/p><p>Alternatively, righteousness is not reversing the proper order of your questions, asking what does not benefit you and abandoning matters of knowledge useful now and hereafter. It belongs to one avoiding this. Enter the houses of knowledge by their doors: ask well, speak courteously, and put first what matters most and benefits most. Fear God: do not alter His rulings or object to His acts, so that you prosper through His enabling and guidance.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: when the Helpers made the greater or lesser pilgrimage, they would say that no roof should intervene between them and heaven until they entered their houses. On returning they climbed walls or opened holes in the backs of their houses. One man entered by the door and was reproached. The Real revealed: righteousness—obedience—is not entering from behind, climbing over or opening a hole above. Righteousness belongs to one avoiding prohibitions and opposing appetites. Alternatively, righteousness is not reversing the proper order of your questions, asking what does not benefit you and abandoning matters of knowledge useful now and hereafter. It belongs to one avoiding this. Enter the houses of knowledge by their doors: ask well, speak courteously, and put first what matters most and benefits most. Fear God: do not alter His rulings or object to His acts, so that you prosper through His enabling and guidance."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"The three houses and their nine doors","html":"<p>Know that the aspirant enters three houses: Sacred Law (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الشريعة<\/bdi>), Path (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الطريقة<\/bdi>) and Reality (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الحقيقة<\/bdi>). Each has doors. Whoever comes by the door enters; whoever comes otherwise is expelled.<\/p><p>The house of Sacred Law has three doors. First is repentance. Once entered and realized with its pillars and conditions, the door of uprightness (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الاستقامة<\/bdi>) meets him: following the Messenger in words, acts and states. Upon realizing it, he meets God-fearing vigilance (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">التقوى<\/bdi>) in its divisions. Realizing this outwardly and inwardly, he enters the purified Sacred Law’s house and ranges through its beauties and meanings.<\/p><p>He then seeks the Path’s house, also with three doors. First is sincerity (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الإخلاص<\/bdi>): directing action to God alone without reservation or personal share. Once realized, the door of emptying (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">التخلية<\/bdi>) meets him: purification from inward faults, which are innumerable. Whoever finds a shaykh is shown them and taught their remedies. Then comes adornment (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">التحلية<\/bdi>): patience, forbearance, truthfulness, tranquillity, generosity, preferring others and every other perfection. Realizing sincerity, emptying and adornment, he has realized the Path’s house.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-133\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B128]<\/a><\/p><p>The house of Reality then meets him. First he knocks at watchfulness (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المراقبة<\/bdi>): guarding heart and secret from bad thoughts. Once the heart is purified of thoughts settled within it, it overlooks the door of witnessing (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المشاهدة<\/bdi>): effacing traces in witnessing the lights of the Living, Self-Subsisting, or rendering vessels subtle when meanings appear. Entering and settling there, he meets the door of gnosis (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المعرفة<\/bdi>), the place of rootedness and establishment, the goal and end. The house of Reality is the mosque of the divine presence. Beyond it remains only ascent in stations and increasing gnoses and unveilings, for ever and ever. May God grant us an abundant share through His grace and generosity.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"Know that the aspirant enters three houses: Sacred Law ( الشريعة ), Path ( الطريقة ) and Reality ( الحقيقة ). Each has doors. Whoever comes by the door enters; whoever comes otherwise is expelled. The house of Sacred Law has three doors. First is repentance. Once entered and realized with its pillars and conditions, the door of uprightness ( الاستقامة ) meets him: following the Messenger in words, acts and states. Upon realizing it, he meets God-fearing vigilance ( التقوى ) in its divisions. Realizing this outwardly and inwardly, he enters the purified Sacred Law’s house and ranges through its beauties and meanings. He then seeks the Path’s house, also with three doors. First is sincerity ( الإخلاص ): directing action to God alone without reservation or personal share. Once realized, the door of emptying ( التخلية ) meets him: purification from inward faults, which are innumerable. Whoever finds a shaykh is shown them and taught their remedies. Then comes adornment ( التحلية ): patience, forbearance, truthfulness, tranquillity, generosity, preferring others and every other perfection. Realizing sincerity, emptying and adornment, he has realized the Path’s house. [B128] The house of Reality then meets him. First he knocks at watchfulness ( المراقبة ): guarding heart and secret from bad thoughts. Once the heart is purified of thoughts settled within it, it overlooks the door of witnessing ( المشاهدة ): effacing traces in witnessing the lights of the Living, Self-Subsisting, or rendering vessels subtle when meanings appear. Entering and settling there, he meets the door of gnosis ( المعرفة ), the place of rootedness and establishment, the goal and end. The house of Reality is the mosque of the divine presence. Beyond it remains only ascent in stations and increasing gnoses and unveilings, for ever and ever. May God grant us an abundant share through His grace and generosity."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>When pilgrimage was prescribed, unbelievers occupied the Sacred House. He therefore commands struggle against them so that Muslims may make pilgrimage:<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"When pilgrimage was prescribed, unbelievers occupied the Sacred House. He therefore commands struggle against them so that Muslims may make pilgrimage:"}],"cairo_notes":[{"id":"C219.2","page":219,"ar":"في الأصول (غنم)، والصواب: غنمة، كما في أسد الغابة، والإصابة.","en":"The source copies have “Ghanm”; the correct form is “Ghanama,” as in Usd al-ghāba and al-Iṣāba.","anchor":"ثعلبة بن غنمة"}],"english_characters":5910,"arabic_characters":4289},{"id":"q2-190-195","q":"2:190–195","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:190–195 · Conflict, sacred limits and the struggle against inward obstacles","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 222–224","cairo":"I:222–224","source_page_first":222,"source_page_last":224,"ar":"وَقاتِلُوا فِي سَبِيلِ اللَّهِ الَّذِينَ يُقاتِلُونَكُمْ وَلا تَعْتَدُوا إِنَّ اللَّهَ لا يُحِبُّ الْمُعْتَدِينَ (190) وَاقْتُلُوهُمْ حَيْثُ ثَقِفْتُمُوهُمْ وَأَخْرِجُوهُمْ مِنْ حَيْثُ أَخْرَجُوكُمْ وَالْفِتْنَةُ أَشَدُّ مِنَ الْقَتْلِ وَلا تَقْتُلُوهُمْ عِنْدَ الْمَسْجِدِ الْحَرامِ حَتَّى يَقْتُلُوكُمْ فِيهِ فَإِنْ قَتَلُوكُمْ فَاقْتُلُوهُمْ كَذلِكَ جَزاءُ الْكافِرِينَ (191) فَإِنِ انْتَهَوْا فَإِنَّ اللَّهَ غَفُورٌ رَحِيمٌ (192) وَقاتِلُوهُمْ حَتَّى لا تَكُونَ فِتْنَةٌ وَيَكُونَ الدِّينُ لِلَّهِ فَإِنِ انْتَهَوْا فَلا عُدْوانَ إِلاَّ عَلَى الظَّالِمِينَ (193) الشَّهْرُ الْحَرامُ بِالشَّهْرِ الْحَرامِ وَالْحُرُماتُ قِصاصٌ فَمَنِ اعْتَدى عَلَيْكُمْ فَاعْتَدُوا عَلَيْهِ بِمِثْلِ مَا اعْتَدى عَلَيْكُمْ وَاتَّقُوا اللَّهَ وَاعْلَمُوا أَنَّ اللَّهَ مَعَ الْمُتَّقِينَ (194)\nوَأَنْفِقُوا فِي سَبِيلِ اللَّهِ وَلا تُلْقُوا بِأَيْدِيكُمْ إِلَى التَّهْلُكَةِ وَأَحْسِنُوا إِنَّ اللَّهَ يُحِبُّ الْمُحْسِنِينَ (195)\nقلت: (التهلكة) : مصدر هلك- بتشديد اللام- قاله ابن عطية. وضمن (تُلْقُوا) معنى تفضوا، أو تنتهوا، فعدَّاه بإلى، أي: ولا تفضوا بأنفسكم إلى التهلكة. ولا يحتاج إلى زيادة الباء.\nوسبب نزول الآية: أن المشركين صَدُّوا رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم عام الحديبية، وصالحوه على أن يرجع في قابل، فيخلوا له البيت ثلاثة أيام، فرجع لعمرة القضاء، وخاف المسلمون ألا يفوا لهم، فيقاتِلُوا في الحرم والشهر الحرام، وكرهوا ذلك، فنزلت الآية.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: وَقاتِلُوا فِي سَبِيلِ اللَّهِ وإعلاء كلمته الَّذِينَ يُقاتِلُونَكُمْ أي: يبدءونكم بالقتال، وَلا تَعْتَدُوا فتقاتلوهم قبل أن يبدءوكم إِنَّ اللَّهَ لا يُحِبُّ الْمُعْتَدِينَ لا ينصرهم ولا يؤيدهم. ثم نسخ هذا بقوله: وَقاتِلُوا الْمُشْرِكِينَ كَافَّةً ... الآية. وَاقْتُلُوهُمْ حَيْثُ ثَقِفْتُمُوهُمْ أي: وجدتموهم، ولا تتحرجوا من قتالهم في الحرم، فإنهم هم الذين صدوكم وبدأوكم بالإذاية، وَأَخْرِجُوهُمْ من مكة حَيْثُ أَخْرَجُوكُمْ منها، وَالْفِتْنَةُ أي: الكفر الذي هم فيه، أَشَدُّ مِنَ الْقَتْلِ لهم في الحرم، وَلا تُقاتِلُوهُمْ عِنْدَ الْمَسْجِدِ الْحَرامِ ابتداءً حَتَّى يُقاتِلُوكُمْ فِيهِ، فَإِنْ قاتَلُوكُمْ فيه فَاقْتُلُوهُمْ فيه، وفي غيره، كَذلِكَ جَزاءُ الْكافِرِينَ يفعل بهم ما فعلوا بغيرهم، فَإِنِ انْتَهَوْا عن الشرك وأسلموا فَإِنَّ اللَّهَ غَفُورٌ لهم رَحِيمٌ بهم.\nوَقاتِلُوهُمْ حَتَّى لا تَكُونَ فِتْنَةٌ أي: شرك وَيَكُونَ الدِّينُ خالصاً لِلَّهِ بحيث لا يبقى في جزيرة العرب إلا دين واحد، فَإِنِ انْتَهَوْا عن قتالكم، فلا تعتدوا فإنه فَلا عُدْوانَ إِلَّا عَلَى الظَّالِمِينَ إذ لا يحسن أن يظلم إلا من ظلم.\nالقتال الصادر منكم لهم في الشَّهْرُ الْحَرامُ في مقابلة الصد الذي صدر منهم لكم في الشهر الحرام، وَالْحُرُماتُ قِصاصٌ يقتص بعضها من بعض، فكما انتهكوا حرمة الشهر الحرام، بمنعكم من البيت، فانتهكوا حرمتهم بالقتل فيه. فَمَنِ اعْتَدى عَلَيْكُمْ بالقتال في الأشهر الحُرُم، أو في الحرَم فَاعْتَدُوا عَلَيْهِ بِمِثْلِ مَا اعْتَدى عَلَيْكُمْ، وَاتَّقُوا اللَّهَ فلا تنتصروا لنفوسكم، وَاعْلَمُوا أَنَّ اللَّهَ مَعَ الْمُتَّقِينَ بالحفظ والتأييد.\nوَأَنْفِقُوا فِي سَبِيلِ اللَّهِ في جهاد عدوكم، ولا تمسكوا عن الإنفاق فيه فتلقوا بِأَيْدِيكُمْ أي: بأنفسكم إِلَى التَّهْلُكَةِ أي: الهلكة فيستولي عليكم عدوكم.\nرُوِيَ عن أبي أيوب الأنصاري (أنه كان على القسطنطينية، فحمل رجل على عسكر العدو، فقال قوم: ألقى بيده إلى التهلكة، فقال أبو أيوب: لا، إن هذه الآية نزلت في الأنصار، قالوا- لما أعز الله الإسلام وكثر أهله-: لو رجعنا إلى أهلينا وأموالنا نقيم فيها ونصلحها، فأنزل الله فينا وَلا تُلْقُوا بِأَيْدِيكُمْ إِلَى التَّهْلُكَةِ، وأما هذا فهو الذي قال فيه الله تعالى: وَمِنَ النَّاسِ مَنْ يَشْرِي نَفْسَهُ ابْتِغاءَ مَرْضاتِ اللَّهِ....)\nأو: ولا تنفقوا كل أموالكم فتتعرضوا للهلكة، أو الطمع في الخلق، ولكن القصد، وهو الوسط. وَأَحْسِنُوا بالتفضل على المحاويج والمجاهدين إِنَّ اللَّهَ يُحِبُّ الْمُحْسِنِينَ فيحفظهم، ويحفظ عقبهم إلى يوم القيامة.\nالإشارة: اعلم أن أعداء الإنسان التي تقطعه عن حضرة ربه أربعة: النفس والشيطان والدنيا والناس.\nفمجاهدة النفس: بمخالفة هواها، وتحميلها ما يَثقُل عليها حتى ترتاض، ومجاهدة الشيطان: بعصيانه، والاشتغال بالله عنه، فإنه يذوب بذكر الله، ومجاهدة الدنيا: بالزهد فيها، والقناعة بما تيسر منها، ومجاهدة الناس: بالغيبة عنهم والإعراض عنهم في الإقبال والإدبار. فيقول الحق جلّ جلاله للمتوجهين إليه: وَقاتِلُوا فِي سَبِيلِ اللَّهِ الَّذِينَ يُقاتِلُونَكُمْ ويصدونكم عن حضرته، ولا تعتدوا فتشتغلوا بهم عن ذكري، والإقبال عليّ، إِنَّ اللَّهَ لا يُحِبُّ الْمُعْتَدِينَ. بل اقتلوهم حيث تعرضوا لكم فقط، فإذا ظهرت صورة النفس أدبها، ثم غاب في الله عنها، وكذلك بقية القواطع.\nوكان شيخ شيوخنا سيدي علي الجمل رضي الله عنه يقول: (عداوة العدو حقاً هي اشتغالك بمحبة الحبيب حقا، وأما إذا اشتغلت بعداوة العدو فاتتك محبة الحبيب،، ونال العدو مراده منك) . هـ. وأخرجوهم من قلوبكم من حيث أخرجوكم من حضرة ربكم، يعني: كما أخرجوكم من الحضرة في أيام الغفلة، أخرجوهم من قلوبكم في أيام اليقظة. والفتنة بالاشتغال بهم أشد من القتل لهم، ولا تقاتلوهم عند مسجد الحضرة وحال الغيبة في الله، فإن ذلك التفات إلى غير الله، كمن كان مقبلاً عليه حبيبه فجعل يلتفت إلى مَن يكلمه ويشغله عنه. وذلك في غاية الجفاء، حتى يقاتلوكم فيه، ويريدون أن يخرجوكم منه بوسوستهم، فإن قاتلوكم، وخطر على بالكم شيء من وسوستهم، فاقتلوهم بذكر الله، والتعوّذ منهم، فإن الله يكفيكم أمرهم، وينهزمون عنكم، كذلك جزاء الكافرين. فإن انتهوا عنكم، وانقطع عنكم خواطرهم، فغيبوا عنهم فإن الله يستركم عنهم، وقاتلوهم على الدوام حتى لا تكون في قلوبكم فتنة منهم، ويكون التوجه كله لله، لا ينازعه شيء مما سواه، فإن انتهوا عنكم فلا تتعرضوا لهم فإن ذلك عدوان وظلم، فَلا عُدْوانَ إِلَّا عَلَى الظَّالِمِينَ.\nفإن جَنَحَتْ نفسُك إلى حرمة الطاعة الظاهرة كتدريس علم أو جهاد أو صلاة أو غيرها، وأرادت أن تخرجك من حرمة الحضرة القدسية وهي الفكرة والشهود والمعاينة، فقاتلها وأخرجها من حرمة تلك الطاعة، فالحرمات قصاص. فكما أخرجتك من حضرة ربك القدسية أخرِجْها من حضرة الطاعة الحسية إلى الطاعة القلبية. فإن الذرة من أعمال القلوب أفضل من أمثال الجبال من أعمال الجوارح.\nفمَن اعتدى عليكم، في زمن البطالة، فاعتدوا عليه في زمن اليقظة بمثل ما اعتدى عليكم. وكان شيخنا البوزيدى رضي الله عنه يقول: جوروا على نفوسكم بقدر ما جارت عليكم. هـ. أي: اقتلوها بقدر ما قتلتكم بالبعد عن ربكم.\nوكان أيضاً يقول: (جوروا على الوهم قبل أن يجور عليكم) . هـ. واتقوا الله فإن الله يعينكم عليها، وَاعْلَمُوا أَنَّ اللَّهَ مَعَ الْمُتَّقِينَ. وأنفقوا أنفسكم ومهجكم في سبيل الله، بأن تطرحوها في يد الله يفعل بها ما يشاء. وَلا تُلْقُوا بِأَيْدِيكُمْ إِلَى التَّهْلُكَةِ فتدبروا لها، وتختاروا لها، وتعتنوا بشئونها، فإن ذلك غفلة عن ربكم. وَأَحْسِنُوا أي: ادخلوا في مقام الإحسان بأن تعبدوا الله كأنكم ترونه إِنَّ اللَّهَ يُحِبُّ الْمُحْسِنِينَ أي: يقربهم إلى حضرته، ويصطفيهم إلى محبته ومعرفته، خرطنا الله في سلكهم بمنِّه وكرمه.\nثم أمر الحق تعالى بإتمام النسك الذي دخل فيه، وحض على الإخلاص فيه، فقال:","quran_ar":"وَقاتِلُوا فِي سَبِيلِ اللَّهِ الَّذِينَ يُقاتِلُونَكُمْ وَلا تَعْتَدُوا إِنَّ اللَّهَ لا يُحِبُّ الْمُعْتَدِينَ (190) وَاقْتُلُوهُمْ حَيْثُ ثَقِفْتُمُوهُمْ وَأَخْرِجُوهُمْ مِنْ حَيْثُ أَخْرَجُوكُمْ وَالْفِتْنَةُ أَشَدُّ مِنَ الْقَتْلِ وَلا تَقْتُلُوهُمْ عِنْدَ الْمَسْجِدِ الْحَرامِ حَتَّى يَقْتُلُوكُمْ فِيهِ فَإِنْ قَتَلُوكُمْ فَاقْتُلُوهُمْ كَذلِكَ جَزاءُ الْكافِرِينَ (191) فَإِنِ انْتَهَوْا فَإِنَّ اللَّهَ غَفُورٌ رَحِيمٌ (192) وَقاتِلُوهُمْ حَتَّى لا تَكُونَ فِتْنَةٌ وَيَكُونَ الدِّينُ لِلَّهِ فَإِنِ انْتَهَوْا فَلا عُدْوانَ إِلاَّ عَلَى الظَّالِمِينَ (193) الشَّهْرُ الْحَرامُ بِالشَّهْرِ الْحَرامِ وَالْحُرُماتُ قِصاصٌ فَمَنِ اعْتَدى عَلَيْكُمْ فَاعْتَدُوا عَلَيْهِ بِمِثْلِ مَا اعْتَدى عَلَيْكُمْ وَاتَّقُوا اللَّهَ وَاعْلَمُوا أَنَّ اللَّهَ مَعَ الْمُتَّقِينَ (194) وَأَنْفِقُوا فِي سَبِيلِ اللَّهِ وَلا تُلْقُوا بِأَيْدِيكُمْ إِلَى التَّهْلُكَةِ وَأَحْسِنُوا إِنَّ اللَّهَ يُحِبُّ الْمُحْسِنِينَ (195)","quran_en":"190. Fight in God’s way those who fight you, but do not transgress. God does not love transgressors. 191. Kill them wherever you encounter them, and expel them from where they expelled you. Unbelief (fitna) is more grievous than killing. Do not kill them at the Sacred Mosque unless they kill [some of] you there; if they kill [some of] you, kill them. Such is the recompense of unbelievers. 192. If they desist, God is Forgiving, Merciful. 193. Fight them until there is no association of partners with God (fitna) and religion belongs to God. If they desist, there is no aggression except against wrongdoers. 194. The sacred month for the sacred month; violations of sanctities are subject to just retaliation. Whoever transgresses against you, respond to him in the same measure as he transgressed against you. Fear God, and know that God is with the God-fearing. 195. Spend in God’s way, and do not cast yourselves by your own hands into destruction. Do good: God loves those who do good.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"Destruction and the return after al-Ḥudaybiya","html":"<p>[Translator’s note: Cairo I:222 prints three forms of <i>qatala<\/i> (“kill”) in the heading of 2:191; the following exposition uses <i>qātala<\/i> (“fight”). The translation preserves this distinction. The bracketed “some of” makes the collective plural address explicit.]<\/p><p>I say: destruction (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">التهلكة<\/bdi>) is the verbal noun of <i>hallaka<\/i>, with doubled lām, according to Ibn ʿAṭiyya. “Cast” includes the meaning of bringing or leading to, hence it takes <i>ilā<\/i>: do not bring yourselves to destruction. There is no need to regard the bāʾ as redundant.<\/p><p>The occasion was the associators’ barring the Messenger in the year of al-Ḥudaybiya, then making peace on condition that he return the following year and have the House vacated for three days. When he returned for the lesser pilgrimage in fulfilment of the agreement, Muslims feared that the others would break it, leading to fighting in the sanctuary and sacred month. They disliked this, and the passage was revealed.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"[Translator’s note: Cairo I:222 prints three forms of qatala (“kill”) in the heading of 2:191; the following exposition uses qātala (“fight”). The translation preserves this distinction. The bracketed “some of” makes the collective plural address explicit.] I say: destruction ( التهلكة ) is the verbal noun of hallaka , with doubled lām, according to Ibn ʿAṭiyya. “Cast” includes the meaning of bringing or leading to, hence it takes ilā : do not bring yourselves to destruction. There is no need to regard the bāʾ as redundant. The occasion was the associators’ barring the Messenger in the year of al-Ḥudaybiya, then making peace on condition that he return the following year and have the House vacated for three days. When he returned for the lesser pilgrimage in fulfilment of the agreement, Muslims feared that the others would break it, leading to fighting in the sanctuary and sacred month. They disliked this, and the passage was revealed."},{"type":"exposition","title":"Fighting, restraint and reciprocal limits","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: fight in God’s way, raising His word, those who begin fighting you. Do not transgress by beginning before they do. God neither loves, supports nor strengthens transgressors. This was then abrogated by “fight the associators all together” [9:36].<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-134\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B129]<\/a><\/p><p>Kill them wherever you find them and do not hesitate over fighting in the sanctuary, for they barred you and initiated harm. Expel them from Mecca as they expelled you. The <i>fitna<\/i>—their unbelief—is more grievous than killing them in the sanctuary. Do not initiate fighting at the Sacred Mosque until they fight you there; if they do, kill them there and elsewhere. Such is their recompense: they are treated as they treated others. If they abandon association and embrace Islam, God forgives and shows mercy to them.<\/p><p>Fight until there is no association and religion belongs purely to God, so that only one religion remains in the Arabian Peninsula. If they cease fighting you, do not transgress: aggression is only against wrongdoers, for it is not fitting to wrong anyone except one who has wronged.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-135\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B130]<\/a><\/p><p>Your fighting them in the sacred month corresponds to their barring you in a sacred month. Sanctities are reciprocally requited: as they violated the month’s sanctity by keeping you from the House, violate their immunity by fighting in it. Whoever transgresses against you through fighting in sacred months or the sanctuary, respond in equal measure. Fear God and do not act merely to vindicate yourselves. Know that God protects and strengthens the God-fearing.<\/p><p>Spend in God’s way in struggle against the enemy. Do not withhold spending and cast yourselves into destruction, allowing the enemy to overpower you. Abū Ayyūb al-Anṣārī is reported to have been at Constantinople when a man charged the enemy’s army. Some said he had cast himself into destruction. Abū Ayyūb replied: “No. This verse concerned the Helpers. When God strengthened Islam and its followers multiplied, we said, ‘Let us return to our families and properties, remain there and put them in order.’ God revealed to us, ‘Do not cast yourselves into destruction.’ This man, however, is described by ‘Among people is one who sells himself seeking God’s good pleasure’ [2:207].”<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-136\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B131]<\/a><\/p><p>Alternatively: do not spend all your wealth, exposing yourselves to destruction or dependence upon people. Keep to moderation, the middle course. Do good by generously assisting the needy and those struggling in God’s way. God loves the good-doers and preserves them and their descendants until the Resurrection Day.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: fight in God’s way, raising His word, those who begin fighting you. Do not transgress by beginning before they do. God neither loves, supports nor strengthens transgressors. This was then abrogated by “fight the associators all together” [9:36]. [B129] Kill them wherever you find them and do not hesitate over fighting in the sanctuary, for they barred you and initiated harm. Expel them from Mecca as they expelled you. The fitna —their unbelief—is more grievous than killing them in the sanctuary. Do not initiate fighting at the Sacred Mosque until they fight you there; if they do, kill them there and elsewhere. Such is their recompense: they are treated as they treated others. If they abandon association and embrace Islam, God forgives and shows mercy to them. Fight until there is no association and religion belongs purely to God, so that only one religion remains in the Arabian Peninsula. If they cease fighting you, do not transgress: aggression is only against wrongdoers, for it is not fitting to wrong anyone except one who has wronged. [B130] Your fighting them in the sacred month corresponds to their barring you in a sacred month. Sanctities are reciprocally requited: as they violated the month’s sanctity by keeping you from the House, violate their immunity by fighting in it. Whoever transgresses against you through fighting in sacred months or the sanctuary, respond in equal measure. Fear God and do not act merely to vindicate yourselves. Know that God protects and strengthens the God-fearing. Spend in God’s way in struggle against the enemy. Do not withhold spending and cast yourselves into destruction, allowing the enemy to overpower you. Abū Ayyūb al-Anṣārī is reported to have been at Constantinople when a man charged the enemy’s army. Some said he had cast himself into destruction. Abū Ayyūb replied: “No. This verse concerned the Helpers. When God strengthened Islam and its followers multiplied, we said, ‘Let us return to our families and properties, remain there and put them in order.’ God revealed to us, ‘Do not cast yourselves into destruction.’ This man, however, is described by ‘Among people is one who sells himself seeking God’s good pleasure’ [2:207].” [B131] Alternatively: do not spend all your wealth, exposing yourselves to destruction or dependence upon people. Keep to moderation, the middle course. Do good by generously assisting the needy and those struggling in God’s way. God loves the good-doers and preserves them and their descendants until the Resurrection Day."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Four enemies and the danger of becoming occupied with them","html":"<p>Know that four enemies cut a person off from his Lord’s presence: the lower self, Satan, the world and people. Struggle against the lower self opposes its desires and makes it bear what it finds heavy until disciplined. Struggle against Satan disobeys him and becomes occupied with God instead, for remembrance melts him away. Struggle against the world renounces it and is content with what comes easily. Struggle against people becomes absent from them and turns aside from their coming and going.<\/p><p>The Real tells those turning to Him: fight in God’s way those fighting you and barring you from His presence, but do not transgress by becoming occupied with them instead of remembering and turning towards Me. God does not love transgressors. Strike them only where they confront you. When the form of the lower self appears, discipline it, then become absent from it in God; likewise with the remaining obstacles.<\/p><p>Our masters’ master, Sīdī ʿAlī al-Jamal, said: “True enmity towards the enemy is being truly occupied with love of the Beloved. If you become occupied with hating the enemy, you lose love of the Beloved, and the enemy gains his intention.” Expel these obstacles from your hearts as they expelled you from your Lord’s presence: as they expelled you in heedless days, expel them in days of wakefulness. Being tried through occupation with them is more grievous than putting them to death.<\/p><p>Do not fight them at the mosque of the presence while absent in God: that would turn towards other than God, like someone whose Beloved faces him while he turns towards another speaking and distracting him—utter discourtesy. Only when they fight there, trying to drive you out through whisperings, should you act. When their whisperings enter your thoughts, overcome them by remembering God and seeking refuge. God suffices you and they flee. Such is the recompense of unbelievers. If their thoughts cease, become absent from them, and God screens you from them. Persist in struggling until no disturbance from them remains in your hearts and all orientation belongs to God, uncontested by anything else. If they desist, do not provoke them: that would be transgression and wrongdoing. There is no aggression except against wrongdoers.<\/p><p>If your lower self inclines to the sanctity of outward obedience—teaching, struggle, prayer or another act—and tries to remove you from the holy presence’s sanctity, reflection, witnessing and direct vision, resist it and draw it from that outward observance to inward obedience. Sanctities are reciprocally requited. An atom of the heart’s works surpasses mountains of bodily works.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-137\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B132]<\/a><\/p><p>Whoever transgressed against you in idle days, respond in wakeful days with equal measure. Our shaykh al-Būzīdī said: “Be hard upon your lower selves in proportion to their hardness upon you”—put them to death in proportion to their having killed you through distance from your Lord. He also said: “Overcome imagination before it overcomes you.” Fear God, and He will assist you against them; He is with the God-fearing.<\/p><p>Spend your selves and very lives in God’s way, placing them in God’s hand to do with as He wills. Do not cast yourselves into destruction by planning, choosing and attending to their affairs, for that is heedlessness of your Lord. Do good: enter the station of excellence, worshipping God as though you see Him. He loves the good-doers, bringing them near, electing them for His love and knowledge. May He include us among them through His grace and generosity.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"Know that four enemies cut a person off from his Lord’s presence: the lower self, Satan, the world and people. Struggle against the lower self opposes its desires and makes it bear what it finds heavy until disciplined. Struggle against Satan disobeys him and becomes occupied with God instead, for remembrance melts him away. Struggle against the world renounces it and is content with what comes easily. Struggle against people becomes absent from them and turns aside from their coming and going. The Real tells those turning to Him: fight in God’s way those fighting you and barring you from His presence, but do not transgress by becoming occupied with them instead of remembering and turning towards Me. God does not love transgressors. Strike them only where they confront you. When the form of the lower self appears, discipline it, then become absent from it in God; likewise with the remaining obstacles. Our masters’ master, Sīdī ʿAlī al-Jamal, said: “True enmity towards the enemy is being truly occupied with love of the Beloved. If you become occupied with hating the enemy, you lose love of the Beloved, and the enemy gains his intention.” Expel these obstacles from your hearts as they expelled you from your Lord’s presence: as they expelled you in heedless days, expel them in days of wakefulness. Being tried through occupation with them is more grievous than putting them to death. Do not fight them at the mosque of the presence while absent in God: that would turn towards other than God, like someone whose Beloved faces him while he turns towards another speaking and distracting him—utter discourtesy. Only when they fight there, trying to drive you out through whisperings, should you act. When their whisperings enter your thoughts, overcome them by remembering God and seeking refuge. God suffices you and they flee. Such is the recompense of unbelievers. If their thoughts cease, become absent from them, and God screens you from them. Persist in struggling until no disturbance from them remains in your hearts and all orientation belongs to God, uncontested by anything else. If they desist, do not provoke them: that would be transgression and wrongdoing. There is no aggression except against wrongdoers. If your lower self inclines to the sanctity of outward obedience—teaching, struggle, prayer or another act—and tries to remove you from the holy presence’s sanctity, reflection, witnessing and direct vision, resist it and draw it from that outward observance to inward obedience. Sanctities are reciprocally requited. An atom of the heart’s works surpasses mountains of bodily works. [B132] Whoever transgressed against you in idle days, respond in wakeful days with equal measure. Our shaykh al-Būzīdī said: “Be hard upon your lower selves in proportion to their hardness upon you”—put them to death in proportion to their having killed you through distance from your Lord. He also said: “Overcome imagination before it overcomes you.” Fear God, and He will assist you against them; He is with the God-fearing. Spend your selves and very lives in God’s way, placing them in God’s hand to do with as He wills. Do not cast yourselves into destruction by planning, choosing and attending to their affairs, for that is heedlessness of your Lord. Do good: enter the station of excellence, worshipping God as though you see Him. He loves the good-doers, bringing them near, electing them for His love and knowledge. May He include us among them through His grace and generosity."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>The Real commands completion of the rite once entered and urges sincerity in it:<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real commands completion of the rite once entered and urges sincerity in it:"}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":7111,"arabic_characters":6333},{"id":"q2-196","q":"2:196","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:196 · Completing pilgrimage and the offering of the self","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 224–227","cairo":"I:224–227","source_page_first":224,"source_page_last":227,"ar":"وَأَتِمُّوا الْحَجَّ وَالْعُمْرَةَ لِلَّهِ فَإِنْ أُحْصِرْتُمْ فَمَا اسْتَيْسَرَ مِنَ الْهَدْيِ وَلا تَحْلِقُوا رُؤُسَكُمْ حَتَّى يَبْلُغَ الْهَدْيُ مَحِلَّهُ فَمَنْ كانَ مِنْكُمْ مَرِيضاً أَوْ بِهِ أَذىً مِنْ رَأْسِهِ فَفِدْيَةٌ مِنْ صِيامٍ أَوْ صَدَقَةٍ أَوْ نُسُكٍ ...\nقلت: المشهور في اللغة أن أحصر الرباعي: بالمرض، وحصر الثلاثي: بالعدو، وقيل: بالعكس، وقيل: هما سواء. و (ما استيسر) : خبر أو مبتدأ، أي: فالواجب ما استيسر، أو: فعليه ما استيسر.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: وَأَتِمُّوا الْحَجَّ الذي دخلتم فيه، وَالْعُمْرَةَ وجوباً كالصلاة والصوم، ويكون ذلك لِلَّهِ لا رياء ولا سمعة، وإنما خصّ الحج والعمرة بالحض على الإخلاص، لما يسرع إليهما من الخلل أكثر من غيرهما، فمن أفسدهما وجب عليه قضاؤهما، فَإِنْ أُحْصِرْتُمْ ومنعتم من إتمامهما فتحللوا منهما، وعليكم فَمَا اسْتَيْسَرَ مِنَ الْهَدْيِ، وذلك شاة وَلا تَحْلِقُوا رُؤُسَكُمْ أي: لا تتحللوا حَتَّى يَبْلُغَ الْهَدْيُ مَحِلَّهُ، أي: حيث يحل ذبحه، وهو محل الإحصار عند الشافعي، فيذبح فيه بنية التحلل ويُفرق، ومِنَى أو مكة عند مالك، فيُرسله فإذا تحقق أنه وصل وذُبح حل وحلق.\nويحرم على المُحْرِم إزالة الشعث، ولبس المخيط بالعضو، فمن كان مَرِيضاً أَوْ بِهِ أَذىً صُداع أو نحوه، فحلق رأسه، أو لبس ثيابه، فعليه فدية مِنْ صِيامٍ ثلاثة أيام، أَوْ صَدَقَةٍ على ستة مساكين، مُدَّان لكل مسكين، أَوْ نُسُكٍ بشاة فأعلى، فهو مخير بين الثلاثة. والله تعالى أعلم.\nالإشارة: إذا عقد المريد مع ربه عُقْدةً، فالواجب عليه إتمامها حتى يَجْني ثمرتَها، فإذا عقد عقدة المجاهدة فليجاهد نفسه حتى يجني ثمرتها، وهي المشاهدة، وإذا عقد مع الشيخ عقدة الصحبة، فليلزم خدمته حتى يدخله إلى بيت الحضرة، ويشهد له بالترشيد. وهكذا كل من عقد مع الله عقدة يجب عليه إتمامها، فإن أُحصر ومُنع من إتمامها فليفعل ما استيسر من ذبح نفسه وحط رأسه، ولا يُكَلِّفُ اللَّهُ نَفْساً إِلَّا ما آتاها، ولا ينبغي أن يستعجل الفتح قبل إِبَّانِه، فلعلَّه يُعاقَب بحرمانه، فكم من مريد طلب من شيخه أَنْ يُطلعه على سر الربوبية قبل بلوغ محله، فكان ذلك سبب عطبه، فيقال له: ولا تحلق رأسك من شهود السوى حتى يبلغ هَديُ نفسك محله فيذبح، فإذا ذُبِحت النفس وأُجهِز عليها حلق رأسه حينئذٍ من شهود السِّوى، وفي ذلك يقول الششتري رضي الله عنه:\nإنْ تُرِدَ وَصْلَنَا فَمَوْتكَ شَرْطٌ ... لا يَنَالُ الوِصَالَ مَنْ فِيهِ فَضْلَهْ\nفمن كان مريضاً بضعف عزمه، أو به أذى بعدم نهوض حاله، بحيث لم تُسعفْه المقادير في مجاهدة نفسه، فليشتغل بالنسك الظاهر من صيام أو صدقة أو قراءة أو غير ذلك، حتى يَمنَّ عليه العليمُ الحكيمُ. وبالله التوفيق، وهو الهادي إلى سواء الطريق.\nولما ذكر الحق تعالى هدى الإحصار وفديَة الأذى، ذكر هدى التمتع، فقال:\n... فَإِذا أَمِنْتُمْ فَمَنْ تَمَتَّعَ بِالْعُمْرَةِ إِلَى الْحَجِّ فَمَا اسْتَيْسَرَ مِنَ الْهَدْيِ فَمَنْ لَمْ يَجِدْ فَصِيامُ ثَلاثَةِ أَيَّامٍ فِي الْحَجِّ وَسَبْعَةٍ إِذا رَجَعْتُمْ تِلْكَ عَشَرَةٌ كامِلَةٌ ذلِكَ لِمَنْ لَمْ يَكُنْ أَهْلُهُ حاضِرِي الْمَسْجِدِ الْحَرامِ وَاتَّقُوا اللَّهَ وَاعْلَمُوا أَنَّ اللَّهَ شَدِيدُ الْعِقابِ (196)\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: فإذا حصل لكم الأمن من المرض أو العدو، وأردتم الحج فَمَنْ تَمَتَّعَ منكم بِالْعُمْرَةِ إِلَى الْحَجِّ بأن قدَّم العمرة في أشهر الحج، ثم حجّ من عامه، فالواجب عليه فَمَا اسْتَيْسَرَ مِنَ الْهَدْيِ شاة فأعلى لكونه تمتَّعَ بإسقاط أحد السَّفَرَيْن ولم يُفرِد لكل عبادة سفراً مخصوصاً. فَمَنْ لَمْ يَجِدْ الهدي، ولم يقدِر على شرائه، فعليه فَصِيامُ ثَلاثَةِ أَيَّامٍ فِي زمن الْحَجَّ، وهو زمنُ إحرامه إلى وقوفه بعرفة، فإن لم يصم في ذلك الزمان صام أيام التشريق. ثم يصوم سبعة أيام إذا رجَع إلى مكة أو إلى بلده. فتلك عَشَرَةٌ أيام كامِلَةٌ، ولا تتوهموا أن السبعة بدل من الثلاثة، فلذلك صرّح الحقّ تعالى بفَذْلَكة الحساب .\nوهذا الهَدْي أو الصيام إنما يجب على المتمتع إذا لم يكن ساكناً بأهله في مكة أو ذي طَوى، وأما مَن كان (أهلُه حاضري المسجد الحرام) فلا هَدى عليه لأنه يُحرم بالحج من مكة فلم يسقط أحد السفرين، وَاتَّقُوا اللَّهَ في امتثال أوامره، وخصوصاً مناسك الحج لكثرتها وتشعب فروعها، ولذلك أُفردت بالتأليف، وَاعْلَمُوا أَنَّ اللَّهَ شَدِيدُ الْعِقابِ لمن ترك أوامرَه وارتكب نواهيه. وبالله التوفيق.\nالإشارة: يقول الحق جلّ جلاله على طريق الإشارة للمتوجهين إليه: فإذا أمنتُمْ من أعدائكم الذين يقطعونكم عن الوصول إلى حضرتنا، أو أمِنْتُم من الرجوع بعد الوصال، أو من السلب بعد العطاء، وذلك بعد التمكين من شهود أسرار الذات، وأنوار الصفات، إذ الكريم إذا أعطى لا يرجع، فإذا حصل لكم الأَمْن، فمن تمتع بأنوار الشريعة إلى أسرار الحقيقة فعليه ما استطاع من الهدي والسمت الحسَن والخلُق الحَسن لأنه إذ ذاك قد اتصف بصفة الكمال وتصدَّر لتربية الرجال، فمن لم يجد ذلك فليرجع إلى ما تيسر من المجاهدة حتى يتمكن من ذلك الهَدْي الحسن والخلق الحسن، هذا لمن لم يتمكن في الحضرة الأزلية، وأما مَن كان مقيماً بها، عاكفاً في شهود أنوارها، فلا كلام عليه، لأنه قد تولاّه مولاه، وغيَّبَه عن شهود نفسه وهواه، فَأمْرُه كله بالله وإلى الله. جعلنا الله فيهم بمنّه وكرمه،\nلكن لا يغفُل عن التقوى لقوله- عليه الصلاة والسلام: «أنا أعرفُكُم بالله، وأنا أتقاكم له» . وقالوا: «من علامة النهايات الرجوعُ إلى البدايات» . والله تعالى أعلم.\nثم ذكر الحق تعالى ميقات الحج الزمانى، فقال:","quran_ar":"وَأَتِمُّوا الْحَجَّ وَالْعُمْرَةَ لِلَّهِ فَإِنْ أُحْصِرْتُمْ فَمَا اسْتَيْسَرَ مِنَ الْهَدْيِ وَلا تَحْلِقُوا رُؤُسَكُمْ حَتَّى يَبْلُغَ الْهَدْيُ مَحِلَّهُ فَمَنْ كانَ مِنْكُمْ مَرِيضاً أَوْ بِهِ أَذىً مِنْ رَأْسِهِ فَفِدْيَةٌ مِنْ صِيامٍ أَوْ صَدَقَةٍ أَوْ نُسُكٍ فَإِذا أَمِنْتُمْ فَمَنْ تَمَتَّعَ بِالْعُمْرَةِ إِلَى الْحَجِّ فَمَا اسْتَيْسَرَ مِنَ الْهَدْيِ فَمَنْ لَمْ يَجِدْ فَصِيامُ ثَلاثَةِ أَيَّامٍ فِي الْحَجِّ وَسَبْعَةٍ إِذا رَجَعْتُمْ تِلْكَ عَشَرَةٌ كامِلَةٌ ذلِكَ لِمَنْ لَمْ يَكُنْ أَهْلُهُ حاضِرِي الْمَسْجِدِ الْحَرامِ وَاتَّقُوا اللَّهَ وَاعْلَمُوا أَنَّ اللَّهَ شَدِيدُ الْعِقابِ (196)","quran_en":"196. Complete the greater and lesser pilgrimage for God. If you are prevented, then offer whatever sacrificial animal is within reach. Do not shave your heads until the offering reaches its appointed place. Whoever is ill or suffers an affliction of the head owes a ransom of fasting, charity or sacrifice. When you are secure, whoever enjoys the lesser pilgrimage until the greater shall offer whatever sacrificial animal is within reach. Whoever cannot find one shall fast three days during pilgrimage and seven upon your return: ten complete days. This applies to one whose household is not resident at the Sacred Mosque. Fear God, and know that God is severe in punishment.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"Being prevented from completing the rites","html":"<p>I say: the well-known linguistic distinction makes the four-letter form <i>aḥṣara<\/i> refer to illness and the three-letter form <i>ḥaṣara<\/i> to an enemy’s obstruction. Some reverse this, and some make them equivalent. “Whatever is within reach” is a predicate or subject: the obligation is what is within reach, or what is within reach is incumbent upon him.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: the well-known linguistic distinction makes the four-letter form aḥṣara refer to illness and the three-letter form ḥaṣara to an enemy’s obstruction. Some reverse this, and some make them equivalent. “Whatever is within reach” is a predicate or subject: the obligation is what is within reach, or what is within reach is incumbent upon him."},{"type":"exposition","title":"Completion, obstruction and compensation for removing hair","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: complete the greater and lesser pilgrimage you have begun, as an obligation like prayer and fasting. Do so for God, not display or reputation. They are particularly urged to sincerity because corruption enters them more readily than other acts. Whoever invalidates either must make it up.<\/p><p>If prevented from completion, leave the consecrated state and owe whatever offering is available: a sheep. Do not shave—that is, do not leave consecration—until the offering reaches the place where it may be slaughtered. According to al-Shāfiʿī this is the place of obstruction: it is slaughtered there with the intention of leaving consecration and distributed. According to Mālik the place is Minā or Mecca: the offering is sent, and once its arrival and slaughter are established, the pilgrim leaves consecration and shaves.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-138\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B133]<\/a><\/p><p>One in consecration may not remove dishevelment or wear clothing sewn to fit a bodily member. Whoever is ill or suffers a headache or similar affliction and shaves or puts on ordinary clothing owes a ransom: fasting three days, giving two mudds to each of six needy persons, or sacrificing a sheep or larger animal. He chooses among the three. And God Most High knows best.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: complete the greater and lesser pilgrimage you have begun, as an obligation like prayer and fasting. Do so for God, not display or reputation. They are particularly urged to sincerity because corruption enters them more readily than other acts. Whoever invalidates either must make it up. If prevented from completion, leave the consecrated state and owe whatever offering is available: a sheep. Do not shave—that is, do not leave consecration—until the offering reaches the place where it may be slaughtered. According to al-Shāfiʿī this is the place of obstruction: it is slaughtered there with the intention of leaving consecration and distributed. According to Mālik the place is Minā or Mecca: the offering is sent, and once its arrival and slaughter are established, the pilgrim leaves consecration and shaves. [B133] One in consecration may not remove dishevelment or wear clothing sewn to fit a bodily member. Whoever is ill or suffers a headache or similar affliction and shaves or puts on ordinary clothing owes a ransom: fasting three days, giving two mudds to each of six needy persons, or sacrificing a sheep or larger animal. He chooses among the three. And God Most High knows best."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Completing a covenant and not forcing an opening","html":"<p>When an aspirant binds himself to his Lord by a commitment, he must complete it until he harvests its fruit. Having committed to struggle, he must struggle against his self until witnessing results. Having covenanted for companionship with a shaykh, he must continue serving until the shaykh admits him to the house of the presence and attests his maturity. Every covenant with God must be completed.<\/p><p>If obstructed from completion, do whatever is possible of sacrificing the self and lowering the head: “God charges no soul beyond what He has given it” [65:7]. Do not hasten an opening before its season, lest deprivation be the punishment. Many an aspirant asked his shaykh to disclose lordship’s secret before its proper time and thereby met ruin. He is told: do not shave your head of witnessing an other until your self’s offering reaches its place and is slaughtered. Only when the self is fully sacrificed is the head shaved of witnessing otherness. Al-Shushtarī says:<\/p><blockquote class=\"poetry\">If you desire union with us, your death is a condition;<br>one in whom a remnant remains does not attain union.<\/blockquote><p>Whoever is ill through weak resolve or afflicted by a state that will not rise, destiny not assisting his struggle, should occupy himself with outward devotion—fasting, charity, recitation or another act—until the Knowing, Wise grants His favour. Through God is success; He guides to the sound road.<\/p><p>After the obstruction-offering and compensation for affliction, the Real mentions the offering for combining the two pilgrimages:<\/p><div class=\"quranpanel embedded-quran\" data-q=\"2:196b\"><div class=\"meta\">Qurʾānic continuation · 2:196b<\/div><div class=\"quranar\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">فَإِذَا أَمِنْتُمْ فَمَنْ تَمَتَّعَ بِالْعُمْرَةِ إِلَى الْحَجِّ فَمَا اسْتَيْسَرَ مِنَ الْهَدْيِ<\/div><div class=\"quranen\">When you are secure, whoever enjoys the lesser pilgrimage until the greater shall offer whatever sacrificial animal is within reach.<\/div><\/div>","authorial":true,"text":"When an aspirant binds himself to his Lord by a commitment, he must complete it until he harvests its fruit. Having committed to struggle, he must struggle against his self until witnessing results. Having covenanted for companionship with a shaykh, he must continue serving until the shaykh admits him to the house of the presence and attests his maturity. Every covenant with God must be completed. If obstructed from completion, do whatever is possible of sacrificing the self and lowering the head: “God charges no soul beyond what He has given it” [65:7]. Do not hasten an opening before its season, lest deprivation be the punishment. Many an aspirant asked his shaykh to disclose lordship’s secret before its proper time and thereby met ruin. He is told: do not shave your head of witnessing an other until your self’s offering reaches its place and is slaughtered. Only when the self is fully sacrificed is the head shaved of witnessing otherness. Al-Shushtarī says: If you desire union with us, your death is a condition; one in whom a remnant remains does not attain union. Whoever is ill through weak resolve or afflicted by a state that will not rise, destiny not assisting his struggle, should occupy himself with outward devotion—fasting, charity, recitation or another act—until the Knowing, Wise grants His favour. Through God is success; He guides to the sound road. After the obstruction-offering and compensation for affliction, the Real mentions the offering for combining the two pilgrimages: Qurʾānic continuation · 2:196b فَإِذَا أَمِنْتُمْ فَمَنْ تَمَتَّعَ بِالْعُمْرَةِ إِلَى الْحَجِّ فَمَا اسْتَيْسَرَ مِنَ الْهَدْيِ When you are secure, whoever enjoys the lesser pilgrimage until the greater shall offer whatever sacrificial animal is within reach."},{"type":"exposition","title":"Tamattuʿ and the complete count of ten","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: when secure from illness or enemy and intending pilgrimage, whoever performs the lesser pilgrimage in the pilgrimage months, then the greater in the same year, owes an available offering, a sheep or larger animal. He enjoyed the benefit of dispensing with one of two journeys, not assigning each act its own journey.<\/p><p>Whoever finds no offering and cannot buy one must fast three days in pilgrimage’s period, from consecration to standing at ʿArafa. If he does not fast then, he fasts the days of Tashrīq. He then fasts seven when he returns to Mecca or his homeland. These are ten complete days: do not suppose the seven replace the three. Hence the explicit summation (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">فذلكة الحساب<\/bdi>).<\/p><p>The offering or fast applies only when the pilgrim’s household does not reside in Mecca or Dhū Ṭuwā. A resident at the Sacred Mosque owes no offering, since he enters consecration for pilgrimage from Mecca and has not dispensed with a journey. Fear God in obeying His commands, especially the many, ramifying pilgrimage rites, which accordingly receive separate treatises. Know His punishment is severe for abandoning commands and committing prohibitions. Through God is success.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: when secure from illness or enemy and intending pilgrimage, whoever performs the lesser pilgrimage in the pilgrimage months, then the greater in the same year, owes an available offering, a sheep or larger animal. He enjoyed the benefit of dispensing with one of two journeys, not assigning each act its own journey. Whoever finds no offering and cannot buy one must fast three days in pilgrimage’s period, from consecration to standing at ʿArafa. If he does not fast then, he fasts the days of Tashrīq. He then fasts seven when he returns to Mecca or his homeland. These are ten complete days: do not suppose the seven replace the three. Hence the explicit summation ( فذلكة الحساب ). The offering or fast applies only when the pilgrim’s household does not reside in Mecca or Dhū Ṭuwā. A resident at the Sacred Mosque owes no offering, since he enters consecration for pilgrimage from Mecca and has not dispensed with a journey. Fear God in obeying His commands, especially the many, ramifying pilgrimage rites, which accordingly receive separate treatises. Know His punishment is severe for abandoning commands and committing prohibitions. Through God is success."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Good bearing after security in realization","html":"<p>The Real tells those turning to Him by way of allusion: when secure from enemies cutting you off from Our presence, from returning after union, or from loss after a gift—after establishment in witnessing the Essence’s secrets and the Attributes’ lights, for the Generous does not withdraw His gift—whoever enjoys the Sacred Law’s lights unto Reality’s secrets must offer what he can of right conduct, good bearing and good character (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الهدي والسمت الحسن والخلق الحسن<\/bdi>). He has attained perfection and taken his place forming men. Whoever does not possess this should return to whatever struggle is possible until established in good bearing and character.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-139\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B134]<\/a><\/p><p>This concerns one not established in the eternal presence. One residing there, abiding in witnessing its lights, lies beyond such discussion: his Master has taken charge of him and removed him from witnessing self and desire. His affair is wholly through God and towards God. May God place us among them through His grace and generosity.<\/p><p>Yet he must not neglect God-fearing vigilance: the Prophet said, “I am the most knowing of God among you and the most God-fearing.” They say: “A sign of the endings is returning to the beginnings.” And God Most High knows best.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"The Real tells those turning to Him by way of allusion: when secure from enemies cutting you off from Our presence, from returning after union, or from loss after a gift—after establishment in witnessing the Essence’s secrets and the Attributes’ lights, for the Generous does not withdraw His gift—whoever enjoys the Sacred Law’s lights unto Reality’s secrets must offer what he can of right conduct, good bearing and good character ( الهدي والسمت الحسن والخلق الحسن ). He has attained perfection and taken his place forming men. Whoever does not possess this should return to whatever struggle is possible until established in good bearing and character. [B134] This concerns one not established in the eternal presence. One residing there, abiding in witnessing its lights, lies beyond such discussion: his Master has taken charge of him and removed him from witnessing self and desire. His affair is wholly through God and towards God. May God place us among them through His grace and generosity. Yet he must not neglect God-fearing vigilance: the Prophet said, “I am the most knowing of God among you and the most God-fearing.” They say: “A sign of the endings is returning to the beginnings.” And God Most High knows best."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>The Real specifies pilgrimage’s appointed time:<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real specifies pilgrimage’s appointed time:"}],"cairo_notes":[{"id":"C226.1","page":226,"ar":"الفذلكة: مجمل ما فصل وخلاصته.","en":"Fadhlaka: the summation and summary of what has been set out in detail.","anchor":"بفذلكة الحساب"}],"english_characters":5840,"arabic_characters":4680},{"id":"q2-197","q":"2:197","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:197 · Pilgrimage’s months and the heart’s perpetual pilgrimage","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 227–228","cairo":"I:227–228","source_page_first":227,"source_page_last":228,"ar":"الْحَجُّ أَشْهُرٌ مَعْلُوماتٌ فَمَنْ فَرَضَ فِيهِنَّ الْحَجَّ فَلا رَفَثَ وَلا فُسُوقَ وَلا جِدالَ فِي الْحَجِّ وَما تَفْعَلُوا مِنْ خَيْرٍ يَعْلَمْهُ اللَّهُ وَتَزَوَّدُوا فَإِنَّ خَيْرَ الزَّادِ التَّقْوى وَاتَّقُونِ يا أُولِي الْأَلْبابِ (197)\nقلت: (الحج) : مبتدأ، على حذف مضاف، أي: إحرامُ الحج أو فعلُ الحج، و (أشهر) : خبر، وإذا وقع الزمان خبراً عن اسم معنى فإن كان ذلك المعنى واقعاً في كل ذلك الزمان أو جُلِّه تعيَّن رفعُه عند الكوفيين، وترجح عند البصريين إذا كان الزمان نكرة، نحو: السفر يوم. إن كان السفر واقعاً في جميع ذلك اليوم أو في جُلِّه لأنه باستغراقه إياه صار كأنه هو، ويصح: السفر يوماً، أو في يوم. وإن كان ذلك المعنى واقعاً في بعض ذلك الزمان تعيَّن نصبه أو جرّه ب (في) ، نحو: السفر يوم الجمعة، أو في يوم الجمعة وقد يرفع نادراً.\nقال في التسهيل: ويُغني- أيْ: ظرف الزمان- عن خبر اسم معنى مطلقاً، فإن وقع في جميعه، أو في أكثره، وكان نكرة، رُفع غالباً، ولا يمتنع نصبه ولا جره بفي خلافاً للكوفيين. وربما رُفع خبرُ الزمان المُوقَع في بعضه. هـ. ومن ذلك: الْحَجُّ أَشْهُرٌ مَعْلُوماتٌ فإن جلّها تصلح للإحرام.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: وقتُ إحرام الحج أَشْهُرٌ مَعْلُوماتٌ: شوال وذو القعدة وذو الحجة، فمن أحرم قبلها كره عند مالك، وبطل عند الشافعي، فَمَنْ فَرَضَ على نفسه فِيهِنَّ الْحَجَّ فيلزم الأدب والوقار، ويجانبْ شهوة النساء، (فَلا) يقع منه رَفَثَ أي: جماع أو كلام فُحْش، وَلا فُسُوقَ أي: ذنوب، وَلا جِدالَ فِي زمان الْحَجُّ ولو مع المكاري أو الخُدّام، ولا غيره من أنواع الخصام فإنه في حضرة الملك العلاّم.\nوَما تَفْعَلُوا مِنْ خَيْرٍ كحِلْم وصبر وحُسن خلق يَعْلَمْهُ اللَّهُ فاستبقوا الخيرات، وتزودوا قبل هجوم الممات، واتقوا الله حق تقاته فَإِنَّ خَيْرَ الزَّادِ التَّقْوى. أو تزودوا لسفر الحج، ولا تسافروا كلّا على الناس\nفَإِنَّ خَيْرَ الزَّادِ التَّقْوى عن الطمع في الخلق، وَاتَّقُونِ يا أُولِي الْأَلْبابِ، وأفرِدُوني في سركم حتى أفتح لكم الباب، وأدخلَكم مع الأحباب.\nالإشارة: معاملة الأبدان مؤقتة بالأماكن والأزمان، ومعاملة القلوب أو الأرواح غير مؤقتة بزمان مخصوص، ولا مكان مخصوص، فحجَ القلوب، الأزمنةُ كلها له ميقات، والأماكن كلها عرفات، حج القلوب هو العكوف في حضرة علاّم الغيوب، وهي مُسَرْمَدَةٌ على الدوام على مَرِّ الليالي والأيام، فكل وقت عندهم ليلة القدر، وكل مكان عندهم عرفةُ المشرَّفةُ القدر، وأنشدوا:\nلولا شهودُ جمالكم في ذاتي ... ما كنتُ أَرْضَى ساعة بِحَياتي\nما ليلةُ القدرُ الْمُعَظَّمُ شأنُها ... إلاَّ إذا عَمَرَتْ بكُم أوقَاتِي\nإنَّ المحبَّ إذا تمكَّنَ في الهَوَى ... والحب لم يَحْتَجْ إلى ميقاتِ\nوقال آخر :\nكُلُّ وقتٍ مِنْ حَبيبيِ ... قدره كألف حجة\nفازَ مَنْ خلَّى الشَّوَاغِلْ ... وَلِموْلاَه تَوَجَّهْ\nفمَنْ فَرض على قلبه حجَّ الحضرة فليلتزم الأدب والنظرة، والسكوتَ والفكرة، قال تعالى وَخَشَعَتِ الْأَصْواتُ لِلرَّحْمنِ فَلا تَسْمَعُ إِلَّا هَمْساً فلا رفث ولا فسوقَ ولا جدال ولا مراء، إذ مبنى طريقهم على التسليم والرضى، وما تفعلوا من خير فليس على الله بخفي. وتزودوا بتقوى شهود السَّوَى، فَإِنَّ خَيْرَ الزَّادِ التَّقْوى، وجِمَاعُ التقوى هي مخالفة الهَوى، ومحبة المولَى، فهذه تقوى أولي الألباب الذين صفَتْ مِرآة قلوبهم، فأبصروا الرشد والصواب. وبالله التوفيق.\nثم أباح الحق تعالى التجارة فى مواسم الحج، فقال:","quran_ar":"الْحَجُّ أَشْهُرٌ مَعْلُوماتٌ فَمَنْ فَرَضَ فِيهِنَّ الْحَجَّ فَلا رَفَثَ وَلا فُسُوقَ وَلا جِدالَ فِي الْحَجِّ وَما تَفْعَلُوا مِنْ خَيْرٍ يَعْلَمْهُ اللَّهُ وَتَزَوَّدُوا فَإِنَّ خَيْرَ الزَّادِ التَّقْوى وَاتَّقُونِ يا أُولِي الْأَلْبابِ (197)","quran_en":"197. Pilgrimage is in known months. Whoever undertakes pilgrimage in them must abstain from sexual intimacy, wrongdoing and disputation during pilgrimage. Whatever good you do, God knows it. Take provision, for the best provision is God-fearing vigilance. Fear Me, people of understanding.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"A period of time predicated of an abstract noun","html":"<p>I say: “pilgrimage” is a subject with an omitted annexed noun: entering consecration for pilgrimage, or performing pilgrimage. “Months” is its predicate. When time is predicated of an abstract noun (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">اسم معنى<\/bdi>), and the event occupies all or most of that time, the Kufans require the nominative. The Basrans prefer it when the time-expression is indefinite: “the journey is a day,” if travel fills all or most of it. By occupying the whole period, the event is as though identical with it. “The journey [takes] a day” in the accusative, or “is in a day,” is also valid.<\/p><p>If the event occupies only part of the time, the accusative or genitive with <i>fī<\/i> is required: “the journey [occurs on] Friday” or “on Friday,” though the nominative occurs rarely. The <i>Tashīl<\/i> says: a temporal adverb can stand in place of the predicate of an abstract noun without restriction. If the event occurs in all or most of the time and the expression is indefinite, it is generally nominative, but, contrary to the Kufans, neither accusative nor genitive with <i>fī<\/i> is prohibited. Sometimes a time-expression containing the event only partly is nominative. Such is “pilgrimage is known months,” since most of them are suitable for consecration.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: “pilgrimage” is a subject with an omitted annexed noun: entering consecration for pilgrimage, or performing pilgrimage. “Months” is its predicate. When time is predicated of an abstract noun ( اسم معنى ), and the event occupies all or most of that time, the Kufans require the nominative. The Basrans prefer it when the time-expression is indefinite: “the journey is a day,” if travel fills all or most of it. By occupying the whole period, the event is as though identical with it. “The journey [takes] a day” in the accusative, or “is in a day,” is also valid. If the event occupies only part of the time, the accusative or genitive with fī is required: “the journey [occurs on] Friday” or “on Friday,” though the nominative occurs rarely. The Tashīl says: a temporal adverb can stand in place of the predicate of an abstract noun without restriction. If the event occurs in all or most of the time and the expression is indefinite, it is generally nominative, but, contrary to the Kufans, neither accusative nor genitive with fī is prohibited. Sometimes a time-expression containing the event only partly is nominative. Such is “pilgrimage is known months,” since most of them are suitable for consecration."},{"type":"exposition","title":"Courtesy and provision for the journey","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: the time for entering pilgrimage’s consecration is known months: Shawwāl, Dhū al-Qaʿda and Dhū al-Ḥijja. Earlier consecration is disliked according to Mālik and invalid according to al-Shāfiʿī. Whoever imposes pilgrimage upon himself in them must maintain courtesy and dignity and avoid desire for women: neither intercourse or indecent speech, nor sins, nor disputation—even with a hired carrier or servants—or other quarrels should occur. He stands in the presence of the All-Knowing King.<\/p><p>God knows whatever good you do: forbearance, patience and good character. Race towards good deeds and take provision before death overtakes you. Fear God as He deserves, for the best provision is God-fearing vigilance. Or: take provision for the pilgrimage journey, not travelling as a burden upon people,<\/p><p>for the best provision is guarding against coveting what creatures possess. Fear Me, people of understanding, and single Me out in your secret, so that I open the door and admit you among the beloved.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: the time for entering pilgrimage’s consecration is known months: Shawwāl, Dhū al-Qaʿda and Dhū al-Ḥijja. Earlier consecration is disliked according to Mālik and invalid according to al-Shāfiʿī. Whoever imposes pilgrimage upon himself in them must maintain courtesy and dignity and avoid desire for women: neither intercourse or indecent speech, nor sins, nor disputation—even with a hired carrier or servants—or other quarrels should occur. He stands in the presence of the All-Knowing King. God knows whatever good you do: forbearance, patience and good character. Race towards good deeds and take provision before death overtakes you. Fear God as He deserves, for the best provision is God-fearing vigilance. Or: take provision for the pilgrimage journey, not travelling as a burden upon people, for the best provision is guarding against coveting what creatures possess. Fear Me, people of understanding, and single Me out in your secret, so that I open the door and admit you among the beloved."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Every time a Night of Power, every place an ʿArafa","html":"<p>Bodily dealings are assigned places and times. The dealings of hearts and spirits have no particular appointed time or place. For the pilgrimage of hearts, every season is appointed and every place an ʿArafa. Their pilgrimage is abiding in the presence of the Knower of unseen things, eternally continuous through passing nights and days. Every time is for them a Night of Power, every place an ʿArafa of exalted worth. They recite:<\/p><blockquote class=\"poetry\">But for witnessing Your beauty within me,<br>I would not be content with one hour of life.<br>The Night of Power, exalted in worth,<br>is only when my times are filled with You.<br>The lover established in ardour<br>and love needs no appointed season.<\/blockquote><p>Another said:<\/p><blockquote class=\"poetry\">Every moment from my Beloved<br>is worth a thousand years.<br>Successful is one who leaves distractions<br>and turns towards his Master.<\/blockquote><p>Whoever undertakes the presence’s pilgrimage in his heart should maintain courtesy and contemplation, silence and reflection: “Voices are humbled before the Compassionate; you hear only a whisper” [20:108]. No indecent speech, wrongdoing, disputation or contention: their path rests upon surrender and contentment. No good you do is hidden from God. Take provision by guarding against witnessing otherness, for the best provision is God-fearing vigilance. Its sum is opposing desire and loving the Master. Such is the vigilance of people of understanding whose heart-mirrors are purified, so that they perceive guidance and right judgement. Through God is success.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"Bodily dealings are assigned places and times. The dealings of hearts and spirits have no particular appointed time or place. For the pilgrimage of hearts, every season is appointed and every place an ʿArafa. Their pilgrimage is abiding in the presence of the Knower of unseen things, eternally continuous through passing nights and days. Every time is for them a Night of Power, every place an ʿArafa of exalted worth. They recite: But for witnessing Your beauty within me, I would not be content with one hour of life. The Night of Power, exalted in worth, is only when my times are filled with You. The lover established in ardour and love needs no appointed season. Another said: Every moment from my Beloved is worth a thousand years. Successful is one who leaves distractions and turns towards his Master. Whoever undertakes the presence’s pilgrimage in his heart should maintain courtesy and contemplation, silence and reflection: “Voices are humbled before the Compassionate; you hear only a whisper” [20:108]. No indecent speech, wrongdoing, disputation or contention: their path rests upon surrender and contentment. No good you do is hidden from God. Take provision by guarding against witnessing otherness, for the best provision is God-fearing vigilance. Its sum is opposing desire and loving the Master. Such is the vigilance of people of understanding whose heart-mirrors are purified, so that they perceive guidance and right judgement. Through God is success."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>The Real permits trading during pilgrimage’s seasons:<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real permits trading during pilgrimage’s seasons:"}],"cairo_notes":[{"id":"C227.1","page":227,"ar":"المكاري: هو مكري الدواب. ويغلب على الحمار والبغال، وجمعه: مكارون.","en":"Mukārī: a person who hires out riding or pack animals; the term is used especially of hirers of donkeys and mules. Its plural is makārūn.","anchor":"المكاري"},{"id":"C228.1","page":228,"ar":"وهو الششتري.","en":"He is al-Shushtarī.","anchor":"وقال آخر"}],"english_characters":3785,"arabic_characters":2932},{"id":"q2-198-199","q":"2:198–199","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:198–199 · Seeking bounty during pilgrimage; ʿArafāt and the Sacred Monument","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 228–230","cairo":"I:228–230","source_page_first":228,"source_page_last":230,"ar":"لَيْسَ عَلَيْكُمْ جُناحٌ أَنْ تَبْتَغُوا فَضْلاً مِنْ رَبِّكُمْ ...\nقلت: (أن تبتغوا) : على إسقاط حرف الجر، أيْ: في أن تبتغوا، وسبب نزول الآية: أن عُكاظاً ومَجَنّة وذا المجاز- أسماء مواضع- كانت أسواقاً في الجاهلية يعمرونها في مواسم الحج، وكانت معايشهم منها، فلما جاء الإسلام تأثَّمُوا وتحرجوا أن يتجروا فيها، فقال لهم الحق جلّ جلاله: لَيْسَ عَلَيْكُمْ جُناحٌ أي: إثم أو ميل\nعن الصواب، في أَنْ تَبْتَغُوا فَضْلًا مِنْ رَبِّكُمْ أي: عطاء ورزقاً تستفيدونه من التجارة في مواسم حجكم، إذا خلَصَتْ نيتكم، وغلب قصدُ الحج على التجارة.\nوهاهنا قاعدةٌ ذكرها الغزالي في الإحياء، وحاصلها: أن العمل إذا تمحَّض لغير الله فهو سبب المقت والعقاب، وإذا تمحض لله خالصاً فهو سبب القرب والثواب، وإذا امتزج بشوْب من الرياء أو حظوظ النفس فينظر إلى الغالب وقوة الباعث فإن كان باعث الحظ أغلب، سقط، وكان إلى العقوبة أقرب، لكن عقوبته أخف ممن تجرد لغير الله، وإن كان باعث التقرب أغلب، حُط منه بقدر ما فيه من باعث الحظ، وإن تساويا تقاوما وتساقطا وصار العمل لا له ولا عليه.\nثم قال: ويشهد لهذا إجماعُ الأمة على أن مَنْ خرج حاجّاً ومعه تجارة صحَّ حجه وأُثيب عليه. ثم قال:\nوالصواب أنْ يقال: مهما كان الحج هو المحرّك الأصلي، وكان غرضُ التجارة كالتابع، فلا ينفك نفس السفر عن ثواب، ثم طُرِّد هذا الاعتبار في الجهاد باعتبار الغنيمة، يعني: يُنْظر لغالب الباعث وخُلوص القصد، وكذلك الصوم للحِمْية والثواب، ينظر لغالب الباعث.\nقلت: وتطَّرد هذه القاعدة في المعاملات كلها، وجميع الحركات والسكنات والحِرَف وسائر الأسباب، فالخالص من الحظوظ مقبول، والمتمحض للحظوظ مردود، والمَشُوب يُنظر للغالب كما تقدم.\nوقد ذكر شيخ المشايخ سيدي أبو الحسن الشاذلى رضي الله عنه قاعدة أخرى أدقّ من هذه فقال: إذا أكرم الله عبداً في حركاته وسكناته، نصَب له العبودية لله وستر عنه حظوظ نفسه، وجعله يتقلّب في عبوديته، والحظوظ عنه مستورة، مع جَرْي ما قُدِّر له، ولا يلتفت إليها لأنها في معزل عنه، وإذا أهان الله عبداً في حركاته وسكناته، نصب له حظوظ نفسه، وستر عنه عبوديته، فهو يتقلّب في شهواته، وعبودية الله عنه بمعزلِ، وإن كان يجري عليه شيء منها في الظاهر، قال: وهذا باب من الولاية والإهانة. وأما الصِّدِّيقية العظمى، والولاية الكبرى، فالحظوظ والحقوق كلها سواء عند ذوي البصيرة لأنه بالله فيما يأخذ ويترك. هـ.\nالإشارة: العبد لا يستغني عن طلب الزيادة، ولو بلغ من الكمال غاية النهاية، فالقناعة من الله حرمان، واعتقاد بلوغ النهاية نقصان، فليس عليكم جناح أيها العارفون أَن تَبْتَغُواْ فَضْلاً مِّن ربكم زيادة في إيقانكم، وترَقِّياً في معانيكم، إذ كمالات الحق لا نهاية لها، وأسرار الذات لا إحاطة بها، قال تعالى: وَلا يُحِيطُونَ بِهِ عِلْماً. والله وليّ التوفيق.\nثم ذكر الحق تعالى الوقوف بعرفة، والرجوع إلى المزدلفة والمشعر الحرام، فقال:\n... فَإِذا أَفَضْتُمْ مِنْ عَرَفاتٍ فَاذْكُرُوا اللَّهَ عِنْدَ الْمَشْعَرِ الْحَرامِ وَاذْكُرُوهُ كَما هَداكُمْ وَإِنْ كُنْتُمْ مِنْ قَبْلِهِ لَمِنَ الضَّالِّينَ (198) ثُمَّ أَفِيضُوا مِنْ حَيْثُ أَفاضَ النَّاسُ وَاسْتَغْفِرُوا اللَّهَ إِنَّ اللَّهَ غَفُورٌ رَحِيمٌ (199)","quran_ar":"لَيْسَ عَلَيْكُمْ جُناحٌ أَنْ تَبْتَغُوا فَضْلاً مِنْ رَبِّكُمْ فَإِذا أَفَضْتُمْ مِنْ عَرَفاتٍ فَاذْكُرُوا اللَّهَ عِنْدَ الْمَشْعَرِ الْحَرامِ وَاذْكُرُوهُ كَما هَداكُمْ وَإِنْ كُنْتُمْ مِنْ قَبْلِهِ لَمِنَ الضَّالِّينَ (198) ثُمَّ أَفِيضُوا مِنْ حَيْثُ أَفاضَ النَّاسُ وَاسْتَغْفِرُوا اللَّهَ إِنَّ اللَّهَ غَفُورٌ رَحِيمٌ (199)","quran_en":"198. There is no blame upon you for seeking bounty from your Lord. When you pour forth from ʿArafāt, remember God at the Sacred Monument. Remember Him because He has guided you, although before that you were among those astray. 199. Then pour forth from where the people pour forth, and ask God’s forgiveness. God is Forgiving, Merciful.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"Trade during pilgrimage","html":"<p>I say: “That you seek” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أن تبتغوا<\/bdi>) follows the omission of a preposition (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">إسقاط حرف الجر<\/bdi>): that is, “in your seeking.” The occasion of this verse’s revelation was that ʿUkāẓ, Majanna and Dhū al-Majāz—names of places—were markets in the Age of Ignorance. People frequented them in the pilgrimage seasons and drew their livelihoods from them. When Islam came, they felt that trading there was sinful and were reluctant to do so. The Real, glorious is His majesty, therefore told them: there is no blame upon you—no sin or departure<\/p><p>from what is right—in seeking bounty from your Lord: a gift and provision obtained through trade in your pilgrimage seasons, provided your intention is sincere and your purpose of pilgrimage predominates over trade.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: “That you seek” ( أن تبتغوا ) follows the omission of a preposition ( إسقاط حرف الجر ): that is, “in your seeking.” The occasion of this verse’s revelation was that ʿUkāẓ, Majanna and Dhū al-Majāz—names of places—were markets in the Age of Ignorance. People frequented them in the pilgrimage seasons and drew their livelihoods from them. When Islam came, they felt that trading there was sinful and were reluctant to do so. The Real, glorious is His majesty, therefore told them: there is no blame upon you—no sin or departure from what is right—in seeking bounty from your Lord: a gift and provision obtained through trade in your pilgrimage seasons, provided your intention is sincere and your purpose of pilgrimage predominates over trade."},{"type":"exposition","title":"Mixed motives and the predominant impulse","html":"<p>Here there is a rule mentioned by al-Ghazālī in the <i>Iḥyāʾ<\/i>. Its substance is that an act done entirely for something other than God causes divine loathing and punishment; one done wholly and sincerely for God causes nearness and reward. When it is mixed with ostentation (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الرياء<\/bdi>) or the lower self’s interests (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">حظوظ النفس<\/bdi>), consideration is given to what predominates and to the strength of the motive. If the motive of self-interest predominates, the act falls short and is nearer to punishment, although its punishment is lighter than that of an act done wholly for something other than God. If the motive of drawing near to God predominates, the reward is reduced in proportion to the self-interested motive. If the two are equal, they oppose and cancel one another: the act counts neither for nor against its doer.<\/p><p>He then says: “The community’s agreement that someone who sets out on pilgrimage while conducting trade has a valid pilgrimage and receives reward for it bears witness to this.” He continues: “The correct position is to say that whenever pilgrimage is the original impulse and the commercial aim merely follows it, the journey itself is not without reward.” He then applies this consideration to fighting in God’s path in relation to booty: one considers the predominant motive and the sincerity of purpose. Likewise, when fasting is undertaken for a regimen and for reward, one considers the predominant motive.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-140\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B135]<\/a><\/p><p>I say: this rule applies to all dealings, movements and rests, crafts and other means of livelihood. What is free of self-interested aims is accepted; what is wholly directed to such aims is rejected; and in a mixed act one considers what predominates, as already explained.<\/p><p>The master of masters, my lord Abū al-Ḥasan al-Shādhilī, God be pleased with him, stated another rule, subtler than this:<\/p><p>“When God honours a servant in his movements and rests, He sets servanthood to God (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">العبودية لله<\/bdi>) before him and conceals his own interests from him. He moves within his servanthood while his interests remain hidden, although what has been decreed for him continues to run its course. He does not attend to those interests, for they are apart from him. When God abases a servant in his movements and rests, He sets the servant’s own interests before him and conceals his servanthood. He moves within his desires, while servanthood to God is apart from him, although something of it may occur outwardly.”<\/p><p>He says: “This is one aspect of sainthood and abasement. As for the greatest veracity (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الصديقية العظمى<\/bdi>) and the greater sainthood (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الولاية الكبرى<\/bdi>), personal interests and duties are all alike for the person of insight, because he is through God in what he takes and leaves.” End of quotation.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"Here there is a rule mentioned by al-Ghazālī in the Iḥyāʾ . Its substance is that an act done entirely for something other than God causes divine loathing and punishment; one done wholly and sincerely for God causes nearness and reward. When it is mixed with ostentation ( الرياء ) or the lower self’s interests ( حظوظ النفس ), consideration is given to what predominates and to the strength of the motive. If the motive of self-interest predominates, the act falls short and is nearer to punishment, although its punishment is lighter than that of an act done wholly for something other than God. If the motive of drawing near to God predominates, the reward is reduced in proportion to the self-interested motive. If the two are equal, they oppose and cancel one another: the act counts neither for nor against its doer. He then says: “The community’s agreement that someone who sets out on pilgrimage while conducting trade has a valid pilgrimage and receives reward for it bears witness to this.” He continues: “The correct position is to say that whenever pilgrimage is the original impulse and the commercial aim merely follows it, the journey itself is not without reward.” He then applies this consideration to fighting in God’s path in relation to booty: one considers the predominant motive and the sincerity of purpose. Likewise, when fasting is undertaken for a regimen and for reward, one considers the predominant motive. [B135] I say: this rule applies to all dealings, movements and rests, crafts and other means of livelihood. What is free of self-interested aims is accepted; what is wholly directed to such aims is rejected; and in a mixed act one considers what predominates, as already explained. The master of masters, my lord Abū al-Ḥasan al-Shādhilī, God be pleased with him, stated another rule, subtler than this: “When God honours a servant in his movements and rests, He sets servanthood to God ( العبودية لله ) before him and conceals his own interests from him. He moves within his servanthood while his interests remain hidden, although what has been decreed for him continues to run its course. He does not attend to those interests, for they are apart from him. When God abases a servant in his movements and rests, He sets the servant’s own interests before him and conceals his servanthood. He moves within his desires, while servanthood to God is apart from him, although something of it may occur outwardly.” He says: “This is one aspect of sainthood and abasement. As for the greatest veracity ( الصديقية العظمى ) and the greater sainthood ( الولاية الكبرى ), personal interests and duties are all alike for the person of insight, because he is through God in what he takes and leaves.” End of quotation."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"No final limit to increase in knowledge of God","html":"<p>The servant cannot dispense with seeking increase, even though he attain the furthest extremity of perfection. To be content with one’s portion from God is deprivation; to believe one has reached the end is deficiency. There is therefore no blame upon you, gnostics, for seeking bounty from your Lord: increase in your certainty and ascent in your inward realities. The Real’s perfections have no end, and the mysteries of the Essence cannot be encompassed. He says: “They do not encompass Him in knowledge” [20:110]. God is the guardian of success.<\/p><p>The Real then mentions standing at ʿArafa and returning to Muzdalifa and the Sacred Monument:<\/p><div class=\"quranpanel embedded-quran\" data-q=\"2:198b–199\"><div class=\"meta\">Qurʾānic continuation · 2:198b–199<\/div><div class=\"quranar\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">فَإِذَا أَفَضْتُمْ مِنْ عَرَفَاتٍ فَاذْكُرُوا اللَّهَ عِنْدَ الْمَشْعَرِ الْحَرَامِ وَاذْكُرُوهُ كَمَا هَدَاكُمْ وَإِنْ كُنْتُمْ مِنْ قَبْلِهِ لَمِنَ الضَّالِّينَ ۝ ثُمَّ أَفِيضُوا مِنْ حَيْثُ أَفَاضَ النَّاسُ وَاسْتَغْفِرُوا اللَّهَ إِنَّ اللَّهَ غَفُورٌ رَحِيمٌ<\/div><div class=\"quranen\">When you pour forth from ʿArafāt, remember God at the Sacred Monument. Remember Him because He has guided you, although before that you were among those astray. Then pour forth from where the people pour forth, and ask God’s forgiveness. God is Forgiving, Merciful.<\/div><\/div>","authorial":true,"text":"The servant cannot dispense with seeking increase, even though he attain the furthest extremity of perfection. To be content with one’s portion from God is deprivation; to believe one has reached the end is deficiency. There is therefore no blame upon you, gnostics, for seeking bounty from your Lord: increase in your certainty and ascent in your inward realities. The Real’s perfections have no end, and the mysteries of the Essence cannot be encompassed. He says: “They do not encompass Him in knowledge” [20:110]. God is the guardian of success. The Real then mentions standing at ʿArafa and returning to Muzdalifa and the Sacred Monument: Qurʾānic continuation · 2:198b–199 فَإِذَا أَفَضْتُمْ مِنْ عَرَفَاتٍ فَاذْكُرُوا اللَّهَ عِنْدَ الْمَشْعَرِ الْحَرَامِ وَاذْكُرُوهُ كَمَا هَدَاكُمْ وَإِنْ كُنْتُمْ مِنْ قَبْلِهِ لَمِنَ الضَّالِّينَ ۝ ثُمَّ أَفِيضُوا مِنْ حَيْثُ أَفَاضَ النَّاسُ وَاسْتَغْفِرُوا اللَّهَ إِنَّ اللَّهَ غَفُورٌ رَحِيمٌ When you pour forth from ʿArafāt, remember God at the Sacred Monument. Remember Him because He has guided you, although before that you were among those astray. Then pour forth from where the people pour forth, and ask God’s forgiveness. God is Forgiving, Merciful."},{"type":"language","title":"Pouring forth, ʿArafāt and the reason for remembrance","html":"<p>I say: <i>afaḍtum<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أفضتم<\/bdi>) means “you moved forth.” The original meaning of <i>ifāḍa<\/i> is a forceful outpouring, from water’s gushing strongly; it was then used, by way of intensification, for movement forth generally. <i>ʿArafāt<\/i> permits both full declension and its absence (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الصرف وعدمه<\/bdi>), like <i>Adhriʿāt<\/i>. It was named ʿArafāt because Abraham the Friend said to Gabriel, when Gabriel taught him the rites, “I have understood” (<i>ʿaraftu<\/i>), or because Adam recognized Eve there. The <i>kāf<\/i> in “as He guided you” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">كما هداكم<\/bdi>) gives the reason (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">تعليلية<\/bdi>), and <i>mā<\/i> is infinitival (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مصدرية<\/bdi>): remember Him on account of His guidance of you. <i>In<\/i> in “although you were” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">إن كنتم<\/bdi>) is the lightened form (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مخففة<\/bdi>), and the <i>lām<\/i> distinguishes it [from the negative]. “Or more intense” [in 2:200] qualifies an omitted verbal noun (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">نعت لمصدر محذوف<\/bdi>): “or a remembrance more intense,” and so forth.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-141\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B136]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: afaḍtum ( أفضتم ) means “you moved forth.” The original meaning of ifāḍa is a forceful outpouring, from water’s gushing strongly; it was then used, by way of intensification, for movement forth generally. ʿArafāt permits both full declension and its absence ( الصرف وعدمه ), like Adhriʿāt . It was named ʿArafāt because Abraham the Friend said to Gabriel, when Gabriel taught him the rites, “I have understood” ( ʿaraftu ), or because Adam recognized Eve there. The kāf in “as He guided you” ( كما هداكم ) gives the reason ( تعليلية ), and mā is infinitival ( مصدرية ): remember Him on account of His guidance of you. In in “although you were” ( إن كنتم ) is the lightened form ( مخففة ), and the lām distinguishes it [from the negative]. “Or more intense” [in 2:200] qualifies an omitted verbal noun ( نعت لمصدر محذوف ): “or a remembrance more intense,” and so forth. [B136]"},{"type":"exposition","title":"Standing with the people and remembering God","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: when you have stood at ʿArafa and poured forth from it, descend to Muzdalifa and spend the night there. After praying the dawn prayer while it is still dark, stand at the Sacred Monument, a mountain at the end of Muzdalifa. Remember God there by declaring His unity, magnifying Him and reciting the pilgrimage response (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">التهليل والتكبير والتلبية<\/bdi>), until daylight spreads. This is what the Messenger, blessings and peace be upon him, did. Remember Him because He has guided you to the landmarks of His religion, the rites of His pilgrimage and its other observances; or remember Him with a good remembrance, just as He guided you with a good guidance. Before this guidance you were among those astray.<\/p><p>Quraysh used not to stand with the people, considering themselves above them, but stood at Muzdalifa. The Real commanded them to stand with the people: then pour forth, company of Quraysh, from where the people pour forth—completing [the rites] with them and departing from where they depart. Ask God’s forgiveness for your alteration of Abraham’s and Ishmael’s rites. God is Forgiving towards you, Merciful to you, if you repent, return and follow your Messenger.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: when you have stood at ʿArafa and poured forth from it, descend to Muzdalifa and spend the night there. After praying the dawn prayer while it is still dark, stand at the Sacred Monument, a mountain at the end of Muzdalifa. Remember God there by declaring His unity, magnifying Him and reciting the pilgrimage response ( التهليل والتكبير والتلبية ), until daylight spreads. This is what the Messenger, blessings and peace be upon him, did. Remember Him because He has guided you to the landmarks of His religion, the rites of His pilgrimage and its other observances; or remember Him with a good remembrance, just as He guided you with a good guidance. Before this guidance you were among those astray. Quraysh used not to stand with the people, considering themselves above them, but stood at Muzdalifa. The Real commanded them to stand with the people: then pour forth, company of Quraysh, from where the people pour forth—completing [the rites] with them and departing from where they depart. Ask God’s forgiveness for your alteration of Abraham’s and Ishmael’s rites. God is Forgiving towards you, Merciful to you, if you repent, return and follow your Messenger."}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":6789,"arabic_characters":2728},{"id":"q2-200-202","q":"2:200–202","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:200–202 · Remembrance after the rites and the aspirations of those who pray","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 230–232","cairo":"I:230–232","source_page_first":230,"source_page_last":232,"ar":"فَإِذا قَضَيْتُمْ مَناسِكَكُمْ فَاذْكُرُوا اللَّهَ كَذِكْرِكُمْ آباءَكُمْ أَوْ أَشَدَّ ذِكْراً ...\nقلت: (أفضتُم) : دفعتم، وأصل الإفاضة: الدفع بقوة، من فاض الماء إذا نبَع بقوة، ثم استُعمل في مطلق الاندفاع على سبيل المبالغة. و (عرفات) فيها الصرف وعدمه، كأذرعات. وسمى عرفات لقول إبراهيم الخليل عليه السلام لجبريل حين علَّمه المناسك: قد عرفتُ. أو لمعرفة آدم حواءَ فيها. والكاف في (كما هداكم) تعليلية، و (ما) مصدرية، أي: واذكروه لأجل هدايته لكم. و (إن كنتم) مخففة، واللام فارقة، وقوله: (أو أشد) نعت لمصدر محذوف، أي: أو ذكراً أشد ... الخ.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: فإذا وقفتم بعرفة، وأفضتم منها، فانزلوا المزدلفة وبيِتُوا بها، فإذا صليتم الصبح بغلس فقفوا عند الْمَشْعَرِ الْحَرامِ، وهو جبل في آخر المزدلفة، واذكروا الله عنده بالتهليل والتكبير والتلبية إلى الإسفار، هكذا فعل الرسول- عليه الصلاة والسلام-، وَاذْكُرُوهُ لأجل ما هداكم إليه من معالم دينه ومناسك حجه، وغير ذلك من شعائر الدين، أو فاذكروه ذكرا حسنا كما هداكم هداية حسنة، وقد كنتم من قبل هذه الهداية لَمِنَ الضَّالِّينَ.\nوكانت قريش لا تقف مع الناس ترفعاً عليهم، بل تقف بالمزدلفة، فأمرهم الحق جلّ جلاله بالوقوف مع الناس، فقال لهم: ثُمَّ أَفِيضُوا يا معشر قريش مِنْ حَيْثُ أَفاضَ النَّاسُ بأن تَقْضُوا معهم، وتفيضوا من حيث أفاضوا، وَاسْتَغْفِرُوا اللَّهَ في تغييركم مناسك إبراهيم وإسماعيل- عليهما السلام- إِنَّ اللَّهَ غَفُورٌ لكم، رَحِيمٌ بكم إن تبتم ورجعتم واتبعتم رسولكم.\n\nفَإِذا قَضَيْتُمْ مَناسِكَكُمْ وفرغتم من حجكم فَاذْكُرُوا اللَّهَ ذكراً كثيراً كَذِكْرِكُمْ آباءَكُمْ أو ذكراً أَشَدَّ ذِكْراً منهم، حيث كنتم تَذْكُرونهم عند فراغ حجكم بالمفاخَرة، وكانوا إذا فرغوا من حجهم وقَفُوا بِمنى، بين المسجد والجبل، فيذكرون مفاخر آبائهم، ومحاسن أيامهم، فأُمِرُوا أن\nيُبدلوا ذلك بذكر الله، وذكر إحسانه إليهم، وشكر ما أسداه إليهم من مفاخر الدنيا والآخرة، إن آمنوا واتبعوا رسوله صلّى الله عليه وسلم.\nالإشارة: إذا وقَفَت القلوبُ على جَبل عرفة المعارف، وتمكنت من شهود جمال معاني تلك الزخارف، حتى صارت تلك المعاني هي روحَها وسرَّها، وإليها مآلها ومسيرُها، أُمرتْ بالنزول إلى أرض العبودية، والقيام بوظائف الربوبية، شكراً لما هداها إليه من معالم التحقيق، وما أبان لها من منار الطريق، وإن كانت من قبله لمن الضالين عن الوصول إلى رب العالمين. ثم يؤمرون بمخالطة الناس بأشباحهم، وانفرادهم عنهم بأرواحهم. أشباحُهم مع الخلق تسعى، وأرواحهم في الملكوت ترعى، فإذا وقع منهم ميل أو سكون إلى حِسّ فليستغفروا الله إِنَّ اللَّهَ غَفُورٌ رَحِيمٌ. ثم يقال لهم: فإذا قضيتم مناسككم، بأن جمعتم بين مشاهدة الربوبية في باطنكم، والقيام بوظائف العبودية في ظاهركم، فاذكروا الله على كل شيء، وعند كل شيء، وقبل كل شيء، وبعد كل شيء، حتى لا يبقى من الأثر شيء، كما كنتم تذكرون آباءكم وأبناءكم، في حال غفلتكم، بل أشد ذكراً وأعظم وأتم، والله ذو الفضل العظيم.\nثم بين الحق تعالى مقاصد الناس، وهممهم في طلبهم وسعيهم، فقال:\n... فَمِنَ النَّاسِ مَنْ يَقُولُ رَبَّنا آتِنا فِي الدُّنْيا وَما لَهُ فِي الْآخِرَةِ مِنْ خَلاقٍ (200) وَمِنْهُمْ مَنْ يَقُولُ رَبَّنا آتِنا فِي الدُّنْيا حَسَنَةً وَفِي الْآخِرَةِ حَسَنَةً وَقِنا عَذابَ النَّارِ (201) أُولئِكَ لَهُمْ نَصِيبٌ مِمَّا كَسَبُوا وَاللَّهُ سَرِيعُ الْحِسابِ (202)\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله في بيان مقاصد الناس وهممهم في طلبهم في الحج وغيره: فَمِنَ النَّاسِ من قصرت نيته وانحطت همته، يَقُولُ رَبَّنا آتِنا فِي الدُّنْيا ما تشتهيه نفوسنا من حظوظها وشهواتها، وليس له فِي الْآخِرَةِ مِنْ خَلاقٍ أي: نصيب، لأنه عجَّل نصيبه في الدنيا. «إن الله يرزق العبدَ على قدر نيته» وَمِنْهُمْ من أراد كرامة الدنيا وشرف الآخرة يَقُولُ رَبَّنا آتِنا فِي الدُّنْيا حالة حَسَنَةً كالمعرفة، والعافية، والمال الحلال، والزوجة الحسنة، وجميع أنواع الجمال، وَفِي الْآخِرَةِ حَسَنَةً كالنظرة، والحُور العين، والقصور، وجميع أنواع النعيم، وَقِنا عَذابَ النَّارِ بالعفو والمغفرة، وقال سيدنا علي- كرّم الله وجهه-: (الحسنة في الدنيا: المرأة الصالحة، وفي الآخرة: الحَوْرَاء. وعذاب النار في الدنيا: المرأة السوء) وقال الحسن: (الحسنة في الدنيا: العلم والعبادة، وفي الآخرة: الجنة) . وَقِنا عَذابَ النَّارِ: احفظنا من الشهوات والذنوب المؤدية إلى النار، وهذه كلها أمثلة للحالة الحسنة.\nأُولئِكَ الذين طلبوا خيرَ الدارَيْن لَهُمْ نَصِيبٌ وحظٍّ من الجزاء الوافر من أجلْ ما كسبوا من الأعمال الصالحات، وَاللَّهُ سَرِيعُ الْحِسابِ يحاسب عباده على كثرتهم، وكثرة أعمالهم، في مقدار لَمْحة. قيل لعليّ رضي الله عنه: كيف يُحاسب اللهُ عباده في ساعة واحدة؟ فقال: كما يرزقهم في ساعة واحدة. هـ. أو يوشك أن يقيم القيامة ويحاسب عباده؛ فبادِرُوا إلى اغتنام الطاعات، واكتساب الحسنات، قبل هجوم الممات.\nالإشارة: الناس ثلاثة: صاحب همة دَنِيَّة، وذو همة متوسطة، وصاحب همة عالية، أما صاحب الهمة الدنية فهو الذي أنزل همته على الدنيا الدنية، وأكبَّ على جمع حطامها الفانية، فقلبُ هذا خالٍ من حب الحبيب، فما له في الآخرة من نصيب. وأما صاحب الهمة المتوسطة فهو الذي طلب سلامة الدارين، وصلاح الحالين، قد اشتغل في هذه الدار بما ينفعه في دار القرار، ولم ينسَ نصيبه من الدنيا لِيقْضِي ما له فيها من الأوطار، فهذا له في الدنيا حسنة، وهي الكفاية والغنى، وفي الآخرة حسنة، وهي النعمة والسرور والهنا.\nوأما صاحب الهمة العالية فهو الذي رفع همته عن الكونَيْن، وأغمض طَرْفَه عن الالتفات إلى الدارين، بل علَّق همته بمولاه، ولم يقنع بشيء سواه، قد ولّى عن هذه الدار مُغضياً، وأعرض عنها مُولياً، ولم يشغله عن الله شيء، يقول بلسان المقال إظهارا لعبوديته للكبير المتعال: رَبَّنا آتِنا فِي الدُّنْيا حَسَنَةً وهي النظرة والشهود، ورضا الملك الودود، وَفِي الْآخِرَةِ حَسَنَةً وهي اللحوق بأهل الرفيق الأعلى، من المقربين والأنبياء، في حضرة الشهود المؤبد فِي مَقْعَدِ صِدْقٍ عِنْدَ مَلِيكٍ مُقْتَدِرٍ. أتحفَنَا الله من ذلك بحظٍّ وافر، بمنِّه وكرمه، نحن وأحباءَنا أجمعين، آمين.\nثم تمّم الحق تعالى ما بقي من مناسك الحج وهى رمى الجمار أيام منى، فقال:","quran_ar":"فَإِذا قَضَيْتُمْ مَناسِكَكُمْ فَاذْكُرُوا اللَّهَ كَذِكْرِكُمْ آباءَكُمْ أَوْ أَشَدَّ ذِكْراً فَمِنَ النَّاسِ مَنْ يَقُولُ رَبَّنا آتِنا فِي الدُّنْيا وَما لَهُ فِي الْآخِرَةِ مِنْ خَلاقٍ (200) وَمِنْهُمْ مَنْ يَقُولُ رَبَّنا آتِنا فِي الدُّنْيا حَسَنَةً وَفِي الْآخِرَةِ حَسَنَةً وَقِنا عَذابَ النَّارِ (201) أُولئِكَ لَهُمْ نَصِيبٌ مِمَّا كَسَبُوا وَاللَّهُ سَرِيعُ الْحِسابِ (202)","quran_en":"200. When you have completed your rites, remember God as you remember your fathers, or with more intense remembrance. Among people is one who says, “Our Lord, give us in this world”; he has no share in the Hereafter. 201. Among them is one who says, “Our Lord, give us good in this world and good in the Hereafter, and protect us from the punishment of the Fire.” 202. Those will have a share from what they have earned. God is swift in reckoning.","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"Replacing ancestral boasting with remembrance","html":"<p>When you have completed your rites and finished your pilgrimage, remember God abundantly, as you remember your fathers, or with a remembrance more intense than your remembrance of them, when you used to boast of them after completing pilgrimage. On finishing their pilgrimage, they would stand at Minā between the mosque and the mountain, recounting their fathers’ boasts and the splendours of their former days. They were commanded<\/p><p>to replace this with remembrance of God, His beneficence towards them, and gratitude for the distinctions He had bestowed upon them in this world and the Hereafter, should they believe and follow His Messenger, God bless him and grant him peace.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"When you have completed your rites and finished your pilgrimage, remember God abundantly, as you remember your fathers, or with a remembrance more intense than your remembrance of them, when you used to boast of them after completing pilgrimage. On finishing their pilgrimage, they would stand at Minā between the mosque and the mountain, recounting their fathers’ boasts and the splendours of their former days. They were commanded to replace this with remembrance of God, His beneficence towards them, and gratitude for the distinctions He had bestowed upon them in this world and the Hereafter, should they believe and follow His Messenger, God bless him and grant him peace."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Returning from witnessing to servanthood","html":"<p>When hearts stand on the mountain of the ʿArafa of gnoses, and become established in witnessing the beauty of the meanings within those adornments, until those meanings become their spirit and secret—their destination and the goal of their journey—they are commanded to descend to the ground of servanthood and undertake the duties owed to Lordship (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">وظائف الربوبية<\/bdi>). This is gratitude for the landmarks of realization to which He has guided them and the beacons of the Path He has disclosed, although before this they were among those who had strayed from arrival at the Lord of the worlds.<\/p><p>They are then commanded to mingle with people in their bodily forms while remaining apart in their spirits. Their forms move among created beings; their spirits pasture in the spiritual kingdom (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الملكوت<\/bdi>). If inclination towards, or repose in, sensory appearance occurs, let them ask God’s forgiveness: God is Forgiving, Merciful. They are then told: when you have completed your rites—uniting inward witnessing of Lordship with outward fulfilment of servanthood’s duties—remember God over everything, at everything, before everything and after everything, until no trace remains. Remember Him as you used to remember your fathers and children during your heedlessness, but with remembrance more intense, greater and more complete. God is possessed of immense bounty.<\/p><p>The Real then explains people’s purposes and aspirations in what they seek and strive for:<\/p><div class=\"quranpanel embedded-quran\" data-q=\"2:200b–202\"><div class=\"meta\">Qurʾānic continuation · 2:200b–202<\/div><div class=\"quranar\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">فَمِنَ النَّاسِ مَنْ يَقُولُ رَبَّنَا آتِنَا فِي الدُّنْيَا وَمَا لَهُ فِي الْآخِرَةِ مِنْ خَلَاقٍ ۝ وَمِنْهُمْ مَنْ يَقُولُ رَبَّنَا آتِنَا فِي الدُّنْيَا حَسَنَةً وَفِي الْآخِرَةِ حَسَنَةً وَقِنَا عَذَابَ النَّارِ ۝ أُولَئِكَ لَهُمْ نَصِيبٌ مِمَّا كَسَبُوا وَاللَّهُ سَرِيعُ الْحِسَابِ<\/div><div class=\"quranen\">Among people is one who says, “Our Lord, give us in this world”; he has no share in the Hereafter. Among them is one who says, “Our Lord, give us good in this world and good in the Hereafter, and protect us from the punishment of the Fire.” Those will have a share from what they have earned. God is swift in reckoning.<\/div><\/div>","authorial":true,"text":"When hearts stand on the mountain of the ʿArafa of gnoses, and become established in witnessing the beauty of the meanings within those adornments, until those meanings become their spirit and secret—their destination and the goal of their journey—they are commanded to descend to the ground of servanthood and undertake the duties owed to Lordship ( وظائف الربوبية ). This is gratitude for the landmarks of realization to which He has guided them and the beacons of the Path He has disclosed, although before this they were among those who had strayed from arrival at the Lord of the worlds. They are then commanded to mingle with people in their bodily forms while remaining apart in their spirits. Their forms move among created beings; their spirits pasture in the spiritual kingdom ( الملكوت ). If inclination towards, or repose in, sensory appearance occurs, let them ask God’s forgiveness: God is Forgiving, Merciful. They are then told: when you have completed your rites—uniting inward witnessing of Lordship with outward fulfilment of servanthood’s duties—remember God over everything, at everything, before everything and after everything, until no trace remains. Remember Him as you used to remember your fathers and children during your heedlessness, but with remembrance more intense, greater and more complete. God is possessed of immense bounty. The Real then explains people’s purposes and aspirations in what they seek and strive for: Qurʾānic continuation · 2:200b–202 فَمِنَ النَّاسِ مَنْ يَقُولُ رَبَّنَا آتِنَا فِي الدُّنْيَا وَمَا لَهُ فِي الْآخِرَةِ مِنْ خَلَاقٍ ۝ وَمِنْهُمْ مَنْ يَقُولُ رَبَّنَا آتِنَا فِي الدُّنْيَا حَسَنَةً وَفِي الْآخِرَةِ حَسَنَةً وَقِنَا عَذَابَ النَّارِ ۝ أُولَئِكَ لَهُمْ نَصِيبٌ مِمَّا كَسَبُوا وَاللَّهُ سَرِيعُ الْحِسَابِ Among people is one who says, “Our Lord, give us in this world”; he has no share in the Hereafter. Among them is one who says, “Our Lord, give us good in this world and good in the Hereafter, and protect us from the punishment of the Fire.” Those will have a share from what they have earned. God is swift in reckoning."},{"type":"exposition","title":"Seeking the good of both abodes","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says, explaining people’s purposes and aspirations in what they seek during pilgrimage and otherwise: among people is one whose intention is narrow and whose aspiration is low, who says, “Our Lord, give us in this world whatever our lower selves desire of their interests and appetites.” He has no share in the Hereafter, for his share has been hastened to him in this world. “God provides for a servant in proportion to his intention.”<\/p><p>Among them is one who desires this world’s honour and the Hereafter’s nobility, who says, “Our Lord, give us a good condition in this world”: gnosis, well-being, lawful wealth, a good wife and every kind of beauty. “And good in the Hereafter”: the vision, the wide-eyed maidens, palaces and every kind of bliss. “Protect us from the punishment of the Fire” through pardon and forgiveness.<\/p><p>Our master ʿAlī, God ennoble his face, said: “Good in this world is the righteous woman; in the Hereafter, the fair-eyed maiden. The punishment of fire in this world is the bad woman.” Al-Ḥasan said: “Good in this world is knowledge and worship; in the Hereafter, the Garden.” “Protect us from the punishment of the Fire” means preserve us from the desires and sins that lead to it. All these are examples of a good condition.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-142\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B137]<\/a><\/p><p>Those who sought the good of both abodes will have a portion and share of abundant recompense because of the righteous works they earned. God is swift in reckoning: despite the multitude of His servants and their deeds, He reckons with them in the space of a glance. ʿAlī, God be pleased with him, was asked: “How does God reckon with His servants in a single hour?” He replied: “Just as He provides for them in a single hour.” End of quotation. Or [the phrase means] that He is about to establish the Resurrection and reckon with His servants. Hasten, therefore, to seize opportunities for obedience and acquire good deeds before death’s sudden onset.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says, explaining people’s purposes and aspirations in what they seek during pilgrimage and otherwise: among people is one whose intention is narrow and whose aspiration is low, who says, “Our Lord, give us in this world whatever our lower selves desire of their interests and appetites.” He has no share in the Hereafter, for his share has been hastened to him in this world. “God provides for a servant in proportion to his intention.” Among them is one who desires this world’s honour and the Hereafter’s nobility, who says, “Our Lord, give us a good condition in this world”: gnosis, well-being, lawful wealth, a good wife and every kind of beauty. “And good in the Hereafter”: the vision, the wide-eyed maidens, palaces and every kind of bliss. “Protect us from the punishment of the Fire” through pardon and forgiveness. Our master ʿAlī, God ennoble his face, said: “Good in this world is the righteous woman; in the Hereafter, the fair-eyed maiden. The punishment of fire in this world is the bad woman.” Al-Ḥasan said: “Good in this world is knowledge and worship; in the Hereafter, the Garden.” “Protect us from the punishment of the Fire” means preserve us from the desires and sins that lead to it. All these are examples of a good condition. [B137] Those who sought the good of both abodes will have a portion and share of abundant recompense because of the righteous works they earned. God is swift in reckoning: despite the multitude of His servants and their deeds, He reckons with them in the space of a glance. ʿAlī, God be pleased with him, was asked: “How does God reckon with His servants in a single hour?” He replied: “Just as He provides for them in a single hour.” End of quotation. Or [the phrase means] that He is about to establish the Resurrection and reckon with His servants. Hasten, therefore, to seize opportunities for obedience and acquire good deeds before death’s sudden onset."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Low, intermediate and high aspiration","html":"<p>People are of three kinds: one with low aspiration, one with intermediate aspiration, and one with high aspiration (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الهمة<\/bdi>). The person of low aspiration has set his aim upon the lowly world and devoted himself to collecting its perishing debris. His heart is empty of love for the Beloved; he has no share in the Hereafter.<\/p><p>The person of intermediate aspiration seeks safety in both abodes and soundness in both conditions. In this abode he occupies himself with what will benefit him in the abode of permanence. He has not forgotten his share of this world, so that he may accomplish his needs within it. His good in this world is sufficiency and freedom from want; his good in the Hereafter is blessing, happiness and delight.<\/p><p>The person of high aspiration raises his aim above the two worlds and closes his eyes to turning towards either abode. He attaches his aspiration to his Master and is satisfied with nothing other than Him. He turns from this abode with lowered gaze, departs from it and turns away; nothing distracts him from God. With the tongue of utterance, manifesting his servanthood to the Great, the Exalted, he says: “Our Lord, give us good in this world”—vision and witnessing, and the approval of the Loving King—“and good in the Hereafter”: joining the company of the Highest Companion, the near ones and the prophets, in the presence of everlasting witnessing, “in a seat of truthfulness, in the presence of an all-powerful King” [54:55]. May God, by His generosity and bounty, bestow an abundant share of this upon us and all our loved ones. Amen.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"People are of three kinds: one with low aspiration, one with intermediate aspiration, and one with high aspiration ( الهمة ). The person of low aspiration has set his aim upon the lowly world and devoted himself to collecting its perishing debris. His heart is empty of love for the Beloved; he has no share in the Hereafter. The person of intermediate aspiration seeks safety in both abodes and soundness in both conditions. In this abode he occupies himself with what will benefit him in the abode of permanence. He has not forgotten his share of this world, so that he may accomplish his needs within it. His good in this world is sufficiency and freedom from want; his good in the Hereafter is blessing, happiness and delight. The person of high aspiration raises his aim above the two worlds and closes his eyes to turning towards either abode. He attaches his aspiration to his Master and is satisfied with nothing other than Him. He turns from this abode with lowered gaze, departs from it and turns away; nothing distracts him from God. With the tongue of utterance, manifesting his servanthood to the Great, the Exalted, he says: “Our Lord, give us good in this world”—vision and witnessing, and the approval of the Loving King—“and good in the Hereafter”: joining the company of the Highest Companion, the near ones and the prophets, in the presence of everlasting witnessing, “in a seat of truthfulness, in the presence of an all-powerful King” [54:55]. May God, by His generosity and bounty, bestow an abundant share of this upon us and all our loved ones. Amen."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>The Real then completes the remaining pilgrimage rites: casting the pebbles during the days at Minā.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real then completes the remaining pilgrimage rites: casting the pebbles during the days at Minā."}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":6394,"arabic_characters":5298},{"id":"q2-203","q":"2:203","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:203 · The numbered days and the freedom to depart or remain","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 232–233","cairo":"I:232–233","source_page_first":232,"source_page_last":233,"ar":"وَاذْكُرُوا اللَّهَ فِي أَيَّامٍ مَعْدُوداتٍ فَمَنْ تَعَجَّلَ فِي يَوْمَيْنِ فَلا إِثْمَ عَلَيْهِ وَمَنْ تَأَخَّرَ فَلا إِثْمَ عَلَيْهِ لِمَنِ اتَّقى وَاتَّقُوا اللَّهَ وَاعْلَمُوا أَنَّكُمْ إِلَيْهِ تُحْشَرُونَ (203)\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: وَاذْكُرُوا اللَّهَ فِي أَيَّامٍ مَعْدُوداتٍ وهي ثاني النَّحْر وثالثُه ورابعُه، وهي أيام التشريق وأيام منى، وأما الأيام المعلومات فهي يوم النحر وثانِيه وثالثُه. والمراد بالذِكْر: التكبيرُ عند الرمي، وذبحِ القَرَابين، وخَلْفَ الصلواتِ الخمس، وغير ذلك، فَمَنْ تَعَجَّلَ فِي يَوْمَيْنِ بحيث رمَى ثانيَ النحر وثالثه، ورجَع، فَلا إِثْمَ عَلَيْهِ وَمَنْ تَأَخَّرَ لرمى رابع النحر، وهو ثالث أيام منى، فَلا إِثْمَ عَلَيْهِ، والقصد بنفي الإثم:\nالتخيير والرد على الجاهلية، فإنَّ منهم مَن أَثم المتعجل، ومنهم من أثم المتأخر. هذا كله لِمَنِ اتَّقى الله في حجه، فَلَمْ يَرْفُثْ، وَلَمْ يَفْسُقْ، فإنه يخرج من ذُنُوبِه كَيَوْمِ وَلَدَتْهُ أمه، كما قال الصادق المصدوق، وَاتَّقُوا اللَّهَ في جميع أموركم، فإنه ذكرٌ وشرفٌ لكم، وَاعْلَمُوا أَنَّكُمْ إِلَيْهِ تُحْشَرُونَ فتجازَوْن على ما أسلفتم من خير أو شر.\nالإشارة: الأيام المعدودات هي أيام الدنيا فإنها قلائل معدودة، وهي كلها كيوم واحد، وأيام البرزخ يومٌ ثانٍ، وأيام البعث وما بعده يوم ثالث، فمن تعجل في يومين، بحيث طوى في نظره أيام الدنيا وأيام البرزخ، وسكن بقلبه في يوم القيامة فلا إثم عليه، وهذا هو صاحب الهمة المتوسطة، ومن تأخر حتى زَهد فى الأيام الثلاثة، وعلق همته بمولاه، ولم يلتفت إلى ما سواه، فلا إثم عليه في ذلك التأخر، إن اتقى شهود السوى، وعلق همته بمحبة المولى، ثم حضّ سبحانه على هذه التقوى فقال: (واتقوا الله) فلا تشهدوا معه سواه، (واعلموا أنكم إليه تحشرون) فترَوْا ما فاز به المتقون.\nولمّا أمر الحق سبحانه عباده بالتقوى ذكر من لم يرفع بذلك رأسا واتبع هواه، ومن امتثل أمره وباع نفسه لله فقال:","quran_ar":"وَاذْكُرُوا اللَّهَ فِي أَيَّامٍ مَعْدُوداتٍ فَمَنْ تَعَجَّلَ فِي يَوْمَيْنِ فَلا إِثْمَ عَلَيْهِ وَمَنْ تَأَخَّرَ فَلا إِثْمَ عَلَيْهِ لِمَنِ اتَّقى وَاتَّقُوا اللَّهَ وَاعْلَمُوا أَنَّكُمْ إِلَيْهِ تُحْشَرُونَ (203)","quran_en":"203. Remember God during numbered days. Whoever hastens to leave in two days incurs no sin, and whoever remains incurs no sin—provided he fears God. Fear God, and know that to Him you will be gathered.","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"The days at Minā","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: remember God during numbered days. These are the second, third and fourth days counting from the Day of Sacrifice: the days of <i>tashrīq<\/i>, the days at Minā. The “known days” [22:28], by contrast, are the Day of Sacrifice and the second and third days counting from it. Remembrance here means magnifying God when casting the pebbles, slaughtering sacrificial offerings, after the five prayers and at other times.<\/p><p>Whoever hastens to leave in two days, casting on the second and third days counting from the Day of Sacrifice and returning, incurs no sin. Whoever remains to cast on the fourth day counting from the sacrifice, which is the third day at Minā, likewise incurs no sin. Negating sin here intends<\/p><p>to establish a choice and refute the people of the Age of Ignorance: some counted early departure sinful, while others counted remaining sinful. All this is for one who fears God during pilgrimage, engaging neither in sexual misconduct nor transgression. He leaves his sins as on the day his mother bore him, as the truthful and confirmed Messenger said. Fear God in all your affairs: it is remembrance and honour for you. Know that to Him you will be gathered and recompensed for whatever good or evil you sent before you.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-143\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B138]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: remember God during numbered days. These are the second, third and fourth days counting from the Day of Sacrifice: the days of tashrīq , the days at Minā. The “known days” [22:28], by contrast, are the Day of Sacrifice and the second and third days counting from it. Remembrance here means magnifying God when casting the pebbles, slaughtering sacrificial offerings, after the five prayers and at other times. Whoever hastens to leave in two days, casting on the second and third days counting from the Day of Sacrifice and returning, incurs no sin. Whoever remains to cast on the fourth day counting from the sacrifice, which is the third day at Minā, likewise incurs no sin. Negating sin here intends to establish a choice and refute the people of the Age of Ignorance: some counted early departure sinful, while others counted remaining sinful. All this is for one who fears God during pilgrimage, engaging neither in sexual misconduct nor transgression. He leaves his sins as on the day his mother bore him, as the truthful and confirmed Messenger said. Fear God in all your affairs: it is remembrance and honour for you. Know that to Him you will be gathered and recompensed for whatever good or evil you sent before you. [B138]"},{"type":"spiritual","title":"The three days of the world, the interval and resurrection","html":"<p>The numbered days are the days of this world, few and numbered. All of them are as one day; the days of the intermediate realm (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">البرزخ<\/bdi>) are a second day; the days of resurrection and what follows it are a third. Whoever hastens in two days—folding away in his sight the days of this world and of the intermediate realm, and dwelling in his heart in the Day of Resurrection—incurs no sin. This is the person of intermediate aspiration.<\/p><p>Whoever remains beyond them, renouncing all three days, attaching his aspiration to his Master and turning to nothing other than Him, incurs no sin in that remaining, provided he guards against witnessing anything other and attaches his aspiration to love of the Master. He then urges this Godfearingness: “Fear God”—witness no other with Him—“and know that to Him you will be gathered.” You will then see what the Godfearing have won.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"The numbered days are the days of this world, few and numbered. All of them are as one day; the days of the intermediate realm ( البرزخ ) are a second day; the days of resurrection and what follows it are a third. Whoever hastens in two days—folding away in his sight the days of this world and of the intermediate realm, and dwelling in his heart in the Day of Resurrection—incurs no sin. This is the person of intermediate aspiration. Whoever remains beyond them, renouncing all three days, attaching his aspiration to his Master and turning to nothing other than Him, incurs no sin in that remaining, provided he guards against witnessing anything other and attaches his aspiration to love of the Master. He then urges this Godfearingness: “Fear God”—witness no other with Him—“and know that to Him you will be gathered.” You will then see what the Godfearing have won."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>When the Real, exalted is He, commanded His servants to fear Him, He mentioned one who paid it no heed but followed his desire, and one who obeyed His command and sold himself to God.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"When the Real, exalted is He, commanded His servants to fear Him, He mentioned one who paid it no heed but followed his desire, and one who obeyed His command and sold himself to God."}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":2329,"arabic_characters":1678}];
